becomes upset\n- From **upset (1)**:\n  - Eat antidotal (x=0) \u2192 becomes healthy\n  - Eat poisonous (x=1) \u2192 **not allowed (death)**\n\nSo we update:\n\n```python\nnew_dp0 = -10**18\nnew_dp1 = -10**18\n\n# from previous healthy state (dp0)\n# skip\nnew_dp0 = max(new_dp0, dp0)\nnew_dp1 = max(new_dp1, dp1)\n\n# eat from healthy\nif x == 0:  # antidotal\n    new_dp0 = max(new_dp0, dp0 + y)  # stays healthy\nelse:  # x == 1, poisonous\n    new_dp1 = max(new_dp1, dp0 + y)  # becomes upset\n\n# eat from upset\nif x == 0:  # antidotal: can eat, becomes healthy\n    new_dp0 = max(new_dp0, dp1 + y)\n# if x == 1: cannot eat from upset (would die)\n\n# skip is already considered (carry over)\n```\n\nWe update `dp0` and `dp1` for each course.\n\nFinally, answer is `max(dp0, dp1)` after all courses, since both states are **alive**.\n\nBut note: if no valid path exists, answer is 0 (can skip all).\n\nHowever, we can always skip everything \u2192 so at least 0 is achievable.\n\nBut note: we are maximizing tastiness, and we are allowed to eat nothing \u2192 so answer \u2265 0? **No**, wait: the problem says \"0 if he eats nothing\", but we are maximizing. But in Sample Input 2, answer is 0, even though all Y_i are negative.\n\nSo we **can skip everything**, and get 0.\n\nBut wait: does \"0 if he eats nothing\" mean that we **must return 0** if he eats nothing? Or is it just stating the value?\n\nActually, the problem says: \"Find the maximum possible sum of tastiness of the courses that he eats (or 0 if he eats nothing)\"\n\nSo if all courses are bad, we can skip all \u2192 total = 0.\n\nBut what if we can get a positive sum? Then we take it.\n\nBut note: we are allowed to skip everything \u2192 so the answer is **at least 0**? Actually, no: the problem says \"or 0 if he eats nothing\" \u2014 but that\u2019s just describing the value. But if we eat nothing, value is 0.\n\nBut we are allowed to skip all \u2192 so we can always achieve 0.\n\nBut what if we can only survive by