Skip: value >10 \u2192 we cannot include it \u2192 so the set of achievable sums remains unchanged\n   - Value v in [1,10]: then new achievable sums = old \u222a (old + v)\n\nWe can do:\n\nLet `dp` be a list of length 2048 (or 2^11), indexed by bitmask representing achievable sums (bit s is set if sum s is achievable). We only care about sums 0..10.\n\nInitialize: `dp[1 << 0] = 1` (only sum 0 is achievable at start)\n\nFor each die i:\n   new_dp = array of zeros\n   For each mask in 0..2047:\n      if dp[mask] == 0: continue\n      Let p = dp[mask]\n\n      // Case 1: die shows value > 10 \u2192 cannot be used\n      prob_skip = max(0, A_i - 10) / A_i\n      new_dp[mask] += p * prob_skip\n\n      // Case 2: die shows v in [1, min(A_i,10)]\n      prob_v = 1 / A_i\n      For v from 1 to min(A_i, 10):\n         new_mask = mask\n         // For each sum s in current mask, s+v becomes achievable\n         for s in 0..10:\n            if mask has bit s:\n               ns = s + v\n               if ns <= 10:\n                  set bit ns in new_mask\n         // So new_mask = mask | (mask << v)  [but capped at 10]\n         shift = mask << v\n         new_mask = mask | (shift & ((1<<11)-1))   # only keep bits 0..10\n\n         new_dp[new_mask] += p * prob_v\n\n   dp = new_dp\n\nAfter all dice, the probability that sum 10 is achievable is sum of `dp[mask]` for all masks with bit 10 set.\n\nBut we are working with **fractions mod 998244353**.\n\nSo we must do all arithmetic mod 998244353.\n\nWe cannot use floating point. We must use modular inverses.\n\nLet MOD = 998244353\n\nWe represent probabilities as integers mod MOD.\n\nWe need:\n- Modular exponentiation for inverses: `pow(x, MOD-2, MOD)`\n\nSteps:\n1. Read input\n2. Initialize dp[1<<0] = 1 (as integer mod MOD)\n3. For each die A_i:\n   - total = A_i\n   - skip_count = max(0, A_i - 10)\n   - prob_skip = skip_count * inv(total, MOD) mod MOD\n   - prob_v = inv(total, MOD)   # for each v in 1..min(A_i,10)\n\n   - new_dp = [0]*2048\n   - For each mask in 0..2047:\n        if dp[mask] == 0: continue\n        p = dp[mask]\n\n        # Case 1: skip (value > 10)\n        new_dp[mask] = (new_dp[mask] + p * prob_skip) % MOD\n\n        # Case 2: value v from 1 to min(A_i,10)\n        nv = min(A_i, 10)\n        for v in range(1, nv+1):\n            # Compute new_mask = mask | (mask << v), but only keep bits 0..10\n            shifted = (mask << v)\n            new_mask = mask | shifted\n            new_mask &= (1 << 11) - 1   # keep bits 0..10\n\n            new_dp[new_mask] = (new_dp[new_mask] + p * prob_v) % MOD\n\n   - dp = new_dp\n\n4. ans = sum( dp[mask] for mask in range(2048) if mask & (1<<10) ) % MOD\n\nBut wait: is this correct?\n\nYes: we are accumulating the probability that the set of achievable sums is `mask`, and we want those where 10 is achievable.\n\nBut note: the die outcomes are independent, and we are branching on each possible value.\n\nBut the state space is 2048, and N \u2264 100, min(A_i,10) \u2264 10 \u2192 inner loop 10 \u2192 total operations ~ 100 * 2048 * 10 \u2248 2e6 \u2192 acceptable.\n\nLet\u2019s test on sample: N=4, A = [1,7,2,9]\n\nBut A[0]=1: only value 1 \u2192 nv=1\nA[1]=7: nv=7\nA[2]=2: nv=2\nA[3]=9: nv=9\n\nBut we must compute everything mod 998244353.\n\nWe\u2019ll code accordingly.\n\nBut note: we must compute modular inverses.\n\nLet\u2019s write the code accordingly.\n\nOne more thing: the initial state: only sum 0 is achievable \u2192 mask = 1 << 0 = 1.\n\nLet\u2019s run the sample manually?\n\nBut easier to code and test.\n\nWe\u2019ll code it.\n\nNote: The total probability space is over all dice rolls. Our DP is summing probabilities correctly.\n\nLet\u2019s code.\n\nWe must be careful: when shifting mask, if s+v > 10, we ignore.\n\nSo we do: `shifted = (mask << v)`, then `new_mask = mask | shifted`, then `new_mask &= (1<<11)-1`\n\nYes.\n\nNow, implement.\n\nNote: We are working in integers mod MOD. The probabilities are fractions, but we represent them as integers mod MOD using inverses.\n\nWe assume all divisions are replaced by multiplication with modular inverse.\n\nLet\u2019s code accordingly.\n\n```python\nMOD = 998244353\n\ndef main():\n    import sys\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    N = int(data[0])\n    A = list(map(int, data[1:1+N]))\n    \n    # dp[mask] = probability (mod MOD) that achievable sums are represented by mask\n    dp = [0] * (1 << 11)  # 11 bits: sums 0 to 10\n    dp[1 << 0] = 1  # only sum 0 is achievable initially\n\n    for a in A:\n        new_dp = [0] * (1 << 11)\n        total_inv = pow(a, MOD-2, MOD)\n        \n        # Probability of rolling a number > 10\n        skip_count = max(0, a - 10)\n        prob_skip = (skip_count * total_inv) % MOD\n        \n        # Probability for each v in [1, min(a,10)]\n        nv = min(a, 10)\n        prob_v = total_inv  # each v has prob 1/a\n        \n        for mask in range(1 << 11):\n            if dp[mask] == 0:\n                continue\n            p = dp[mask]\n            \n            # Case 1: die shows value > 10, cannot be used\n            new_dp[mask] = (new_dp[mask] + p * prob_skip) % MOD\n            \n            # Case 2: die shows v in [1, min(a,10)]\n            for v in range(1, nv+1):\n                # Compute new_mask: mask OR (mask << v), then cap to 11 bits\n                shifted = (mask << v)\n                new_mask = mask | shifted\n                new_mask &= (1 << 11) - 1  # keep bits 0 to 10\n                \n                new_dp[new_mask] = (new_dp[new_mask] + p * prob_v) % MOD\n        \n        dp = new_dp\n\n    # Sum probabilities of all masks where sum 10 is achievable (bit 10 set)\n    target_bit = 1 << 10\n    ans = 0\n    for mask in range(1 << 11):\n        if mask & target_bit:\n            ans = (ans + dp[mask]) % MOD\n    \n    print(ans)\n\nmain()\n```\n\nLet\u2019s test with sample input:\n\nInput: \n4\n1 7 2 9\n\nExpected output: 942786334\n\nWe can run locally or reason