 at pos i (1-indexed)\n    def get_char(i):\n        orig = 1 if S[i - 1] == '1' else 0\n        total_flips = fenw_flip.query(i)  # total additive flips at i\n        return (orig + total_flips) % 2\n    \n    # Initialize bad_arr for positions 1 to N-1\n    for i in range(1, N):  # i from 1 to N-1\n        c1 = get_char(i)\n        c2 = get_char(i + 1)\n        if c1 == c2:\n            bad_arr[i] = 1\n            fenw_bad.update(i, 1)\n        else:\n            bad_arr[i] = 0\n    \n    out_lines = []\n    \n    for _ in range(Q):\n        t = int(next(it)); L = int(next(it)); R = int(next(it))\n        if t == 1:\n            # Flip [L, R]: update difference array\n            fenw_flip.update(L, 1)\n            fenw_flip.update(R + 1, -1)  # safe because fenw_flip size N+10\n            \n            # Update bad_arr at positions: L-1 and R (if in [1, N-1])\n            for pos in [L - 1, R]:\n                if 1 <= pos <= N - 1:\n                    old_val = bad_arr[pos]\n                    c1 = get_char(pos)\n                    c2 = get_char(pos + 1)\n                    new_val = 1 if c1 == c2 else 0\n                    if old_val != new_val:\n                        delta = new_val - old_val\n                        fenw_bad.update(pos, delta)\n                        bad_arr[pos] = new_val\n        else:  # t == 2\n            if L == R:\n                out_lines.append(\"Yes\")\n            else:\n                # Query the sum of bad_arr from L to R-1\n                total_bad = fenw_bad.range_query(L, R - 1)\n                if total_bad > 0:\n                    out_lines.append(\"No\")\n                else:\n                    out_lines.append(\"Yes\")\n    \n    print('\\n'.join(out_lines))\n\nmain()\n```\n\nThis should work.\n\nNote: We assume that `fenw_flip` can handle index `R+1` even if `R+1 > N`, because we made it `N+10`. And in `query(i)`, if i>n, we still compute prefix sum correctly as long as we don't access out of bounds. Our `update` checks `i<=self.n`, so if `R+1 > n`, it won't update. But we set `n=N+10`, so `R+1 <= N+1 <= N+10` (since R<=N), so safe.\n\nRun with sample inputs.\n\nI think it's correct.", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 3064, "output_tokens": 35913, "total_tokens": 38977}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "LiveCodeBench_297", "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nInitially, there are N sizes of slimes.\nSpecifically, for each 1\\leq i\\leq N, there are C_i slimes of size S_i.\nTakahashi can repeat slime synthesis any number of times (possibly zero) in any order.\nSlime synthesis is performed as follows.\n\n- Choose two slimes of the same size. Let this size be X, and a new slime of size 2X appears. Then, the two original slimes disappear.\n\nTakahashi wants to minimize the number of slimes.\nWhat is the minimum number of slimes he can end up with by an optimal sequence of syntheses?\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN\nS_1 C_1\nS_2 C_2\n\\vdots\nS_N C_N\n\nOutput\n\nPrint the minimum possible number of slimes after Takahashi has repeated the synthesis.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 1\\leq N\\leq 10^5\n- 1\\leq S_i\\leq 10^9\n- 1\\leq C_i\\leq 10^9\n- S_1,S_2,\\ldots,S_N are all different.\n- All input values are integers.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n3\n3 3\n5 1\n6 1\n\nSample Output 1\n\n3\n\nInitially, there are three slimes of size 3, one of size 5, and one of size 6.\nTakahashi can perform the synthesis twice as follows:\n\n- First, perform the synthesis by choosing two slimes of size 3. There will be one slime of size 3, one of size 5, and two of size 6.\n- Next, perform the synthesis by choosing two slimes of size 6. There will be one slime of size 3, one of size 5, and one of size 12.\n\nNo matter how he repeats the synthesis from the initial state, he cannot reduce the number of slimes to 2 or less, so you should print 3.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n3\n1 1\n2 1\n3 1\n\nSample Output 2\n\n3\n\nHe cannot perform the synthesis.\n\nSample Input 3\n\n1\n1000000000 1000000000\n\nSample Output 3\n\n13", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "We are given N types of slimes, each with a size S_i and count C_i. The operation allowed is: **choose two slimes of the same size X**, which results in a **new slime of size 2X**, and the two original slimes disappear.\n\nOur goal is to **minimize the total number of slimes** after performing any number of such operations in any order.\n\n---\n\n### Key Insight\n\nThis is essentially a **binary representation** problem in disguise.\n\nThink of it like this:\n\n- Each slime of size S can be \"merged\" with another of the same size to form a slime of size 2S.\n- This is analogous to **binary carry**: two slimes of size X \u2192 one slime of size 2X.\n\nBut note: sizes can be arbitrary and not powers of 2. However, **the merging process is independent across different \"chains\" of doubling**.\n\nBut here's the catch: **only slimes of the same size can be merged**, and merging produces a slime of **double the size**, so it might be mergeable later if there's another of the same new size.\n\nSo we can simulate this using a **dictionary (or map)** to track how many slimes exist at each size, and then **simulate merging from smallest to largest sizes**, because merging smaller ones may produce larger ones that can be merged further.\n\nHowever, since sizes can be large (up to 1e9), and counts can be up to 1e9, we must be careful.\n\nBut the key idea:\n\n> For each distinct size, we can **carry over** the number of slimes that can be merged into the next level (i.e., size doubles), just like in binary representation.\n\nBut wait: **can we treat each size independently?** No, because merging doubles the size, so we need to process sizes in increasing order.\n\nSo plan:\n\n1. Read input.\n2. Store (S_i, C_i) in a dictionary: `count[size] = count`.\n3. Use a dictionary (or map) to simulate merging.\n4. Process sizes in increasing order, and for each size:\n   - The number of **pairs** at this size is `count[size] // 2`\n   - Each pair produces one slime of size `2 * size`\n   - So we add `count[size] // 2` to `count[2 * size]`\n   - The number of **leftover** slimes at this size is `count[size] % 2`, which contributes to the final total.\n5. But note: when we create new slimes at `2 * size`, we may need to process that size later. So we must process in sorted order, and possibly insert new keys.\n\nBut since sizes can become very large, we must be careful not to go on forever. However, note that **each merge reduces the total number of slimes by 1**, and initial total slimes is at most 10^5 * 10^9 = 10^14, so we can\u2019t simulate each merge.\n\nBut we can simulate **per size group** using a while loop over active sizes.\n\nBetter approach:\n\n- Use a dictionary for counts.\n- Use a min-heap (priority queue) or just sort keys, but since we may add new keys, we can:\n  - Use a dictionary and process keys in sorted order, and for each key, process it and possibly add a new key at `2*size`.\n\nBut note: merging at size X produces slimes at 2X, which might then be merged with existing ones at 2X, and so on.\n\nSo we can:\n\n- Use a dictionary `cnt` to store count per size.\n- Extract all keys, sort them, but also new keys may appear.\n\nAlternatively, we can use a **heap** to process sizes in increasing order.\n\nBut since the number of distinct sizes is not too large (initially N \u2264 1e5), and each merge creates a new size, but the number of times we can double is logarithmic (since size grows exponentially), the total number of new sizes is O(total_bits), so overall manageable.\n\nBut worst-case: each merge creates a new size, but total number of merges is at most total initial slimes minus final slimes, which can be huge.\n\nBut we can avoid simulating each merge by using **binary representation** insight.\n\n---\n\n### Better Insight: Represent counts in terms of powers of two\n\nBut note: merging is like **binary carry**, but **each size is independent in its doubling chain**.\n\nWait: **each initial slime of size S contributes to a unique \"doubling chain\" starting at S, then 2S, 4S, etc.**\n\nBut different S may interfere? For example, if we have size 3 and size 6, can they merge? No \u2014 only same size.\n\nBut merging two 3s \u2192 one 6. Then if there was already a 6, now we have two 6s \u2192 can merge to one 12.\n\nSo the chains **can merge** across different initial sizes if their doubling paths **intersect**.\n\nBut the key is: **we can simulate merging from smallest size upward**, and whenever we have multiple slimes at a size, we merge them in pairs and carry over to the next level.\n\nSo algorithm:\n\n1. Use a dictionary `cnt` to store count for each size.\n2. Use a list of keys, sort them, but we may add new keys (doubled sizes).\n3. We can use a while loop: while there are sizes with count \u2265 2, merge them.\n\nBut worst-case, counts are 1e9, and we can't simulate one by one.\n\nBut we can do:\n\nFor a given size `s`, with count `c`:\n- number of leftover = `c % 2`\n- number of merges = `c // 2`\n- so we add `c // 2` to `cnt[2*s]`\n\nBut we must process sizes in increasing order, because a smaller size might generate a larger one, but we don't want to process a large size before a small one that might feed into it.\n\nSo:\n\n- Get all current keys, sort them.\n- For each key in sorted order:\n  - if count[key] >= 2, then:\n    - carry = count[key] // 2\n    - remainder = count[key] % 2\n    - set count[key] = remainder\n    - add carry to count[2 * key]\n    - (and if 2*key wasn't present, we'll process it later)\n- But now, 2*key might be smaller than some keys we already processed? No, because 2*key > key, and we are going in increasing order, so we must process again?\n\nSo we need to **keep processing until no more merges are possible**? But sizes grow exponentially, so depth is logarithmic in max size.\n\nBut worst-case, we might have to do many passes.\n\nAlternatively, we can use a **heap (priority queue)** to always process the smallest size.\n\nSteps:\n\n1. Read input and store in a dictionary `cnt`.\n2. Use a min-heap to keep track of all current sizes that have slimes.\n3. While heap is not empty:\n   - Pop the smallest size `s`.\n   - If `cnt[s] == 0`, skip.\n   - Let `c = cnt[s]`.\n   - If `c >= 2`:\n     - `carry = c // 2`\n     - `rem = c % 2`\n     - Set `cnt[s] = rem`  (but we are popping, so we just leave rem)\n     - Add `carry` to `cnt[2*s]`\n     - Push `2*s` into the heap (if not already there, but pushing duplicate keys is okay if we manage counts)\n   - But note: we might have already processed `2*s` earlier? But since we use a heap and process smallest first, and `2*s > s`, we will process it later.\n   - But we must continue until no more merges? Actually, we can't stop early.\n\nBut the problem: a new size `2*s` might be smaller than some existing sizes but larger than others. The heap ensures we process in order.\n\nBut we may push the same size multiple times? Yes, but that's okay \u2014 we can skip if `cnt[s]` is zero when we pop.\n\nBut we must avoid infinite loop? No, because each time we double the size, and eventually the count will be small.\n\nBut worst-case: we might have many distinct sizes, but the number of operations is bounded by the total number of slimes and the depth of doubling.\n\nBut total number of slimes is decreasing? No, each merge reduces total slimes by 1. But we are not simulating each merge, we are doing bulk processing.\n\nBut here's the catch: we are processing each size only once per \"level\", but a size might be updated multiple times? For example, size 6 might get slimes from size 3 merging, and also have its own initial slimes.\n\nSo we must allow that a size can be pushed multiple times.\n\nBut we can do:\n\n- Use `heapq` for min-heap.\n- Use dictionary `cnt` for counts.\n- While heap not empty:\n  - pop smallest size `s`\n  - if `cnt[s]` is 0, skip\n  - else:\n    - if `cnt[s] >= 2`:\n      - `carry = cnt[s] // 2`\n      - `rem = cnt[s] % 2`\n      - set `cnt[s] = rem`\n      - `cnt[2*s] += carry`\n      - push `2*s` into heap\n    - (and we don't push `s` again because we set it to remainder)\n- Then at the end, sum all `cnt[s]` for s in cnt where cnt[s] > 0.\n\nBut is this correct?\n\nLet\u2019s test with sample 1:\n\nInput:\n3\n3 3\n5 1\n6 1\n\nInitial:\ncnt: {3:3, 5:1, 6:1}\nheap: [3,5,6]\n\nPop s=3, cnt[3]=3\n- carry = 3//2 = 1, rem = 1\n- cnt[3] = 1\n- cnt[6] += 1 \u2192 cnt[6] = 2\n- push 6 (already there, but push again)\n\nNow heap: [5,6,6]\n\nPop s=5, cnt[5]=1 \u2192 no merge, leave as is.\n\nPop s=6, cnt[6]=2\n- carry = 1, rem = 0\n- cnt[6] = 0\n- cnt[12] += 1\n- push 12\n\nNow heap: [6,12] \u2192 next pop s=6, but cnt[6]=0 \u2192 skip\npop s=12, cnt[12]=1 \u2192 no merge\n\nTotal slimes: cnt[3]=1, cnt[5]=1, cnt[12]=1 \u2192 total=3 \u2192 correct.\n\nSample 2:\n1 1\n2 1\n3 1\n\ncnt: {1:1,2:1,3:1}\nheap: [1,2,3]\n\npop 1: cnt=1 \u2192 no merge\npop 2: cnt=1 \u2192 no merge\npop 3: cnt=1 \u2192 no merge\n\ntotal=3 \u2192 correct.\n\nSample 3:\n1000000000 1000000000\n\nWe start with 1e9 slimes of size 1e9.\n\nWe will merge them in pairs, producing 5e8 slimes of size 2e9\nThen merge those: 2.5e8 of size 4e9\nThen 1.25e8 of size 8e9\n...\n\nThis is just **number of ones in binary representation of 1000000000**\n\nWait: each merge is like a binary carry. The **minimum number of slimes** we can get is the **number of ones in the binary representation of the total count in each doubling chain**.\n\nBut here, all slimes start at the same size, so they are in the same chain.\n\nBut when we merge, we are effectively representing the count in binary: each bit corresponds to a power of two in the doubling hierarchy.\n\nBut the final number of slimes is the **number of 1s in the binary representation of the initial count**, because:\n\n- Each time you merge two slimes of size X into one of size 2X, it's like carrying over in binary.\n- The process stops when no size has more than one slime \u2014 i.e., each power in the binary expansion has at most one slime.\n\nBut wait: is that true?\n\nExample: count = 3\n- 3 in binary: 11 \u2192 two ones \u2192 two slimes?\nBut in our earlier example: 3 slimes of size 3 \u2192 merge two \u2192 one of size 3 and one of size 6.\nThen we have two slimes? But then we had another size 6 from input.\n\nBut in this case, no other sizes.\n\nSo: 3 slimes of size X:\n- merge two \u2192 one of size 2X, one of size X\n- now we have one X and one 2X \u2192 total 2 slimes.\n\nBut 3 in binary is 11 \u2192 two ones \u2192 two slimes.\n\nBut in sample 3: count = 1000000000\n\nNumber of ones in binary representation of 1000000000?\n\nBut wait: what if the count is carried over and then merged with other slimes from different initial sizes?\n\nBut in this case, only one size.\n\nSo for a single size S with count C, the final contribution to total slimes is **popcount(C)** \u2014 the number of ones in binary representation of C.\n\nBut is that always true?\n\nWait: when we merge, we are effectively breaking down C into powers of two.\n\nFor example, C = 13 = 8+4+1 \u2192 three slimes: at sizes S*8, S*4, S*1? No.\n\nActually, no: merging always doubles the size.\n\nSo the process:\n\n- Start with 13 slimes of size S.\n- Merge 6 pairs \u2192 6 slimes of size 2S, and 1 leftover of size S.\n- Now 6 at 2S: merge 3 pairs \u2192 3 at 4S\n- 3 at 4S: merge 1 pair \u2192 1 at 8S, 1 leftover at 4S\n- Now we have: S, 4S, 8S \u2192 total 3 slimes.\n\nBut 13 in binary is 1101 \u2192 three ones \u2192 matches.\n\nSo yes: **for a single size, the number of leftover slimes is the number of ones in the binary representation of the count.**\n\nBut what if multiple sizes are involved and their doubling paths **collide**?\n\nExample: we have size 3 and size 6.\n\n- size 3: count 3 \u2192 becomes: one at 3, one at 6 (from 3->6 merge)\n- plus original size 6: count 1 \u2192 so now total at 6: 1 (from merge) + 1 (original) = 2\n- then merge two 6s \u2192 one at 12\n\nFinal: one at 3, one at 12 \u2192 total 2? But earlier we had output 3.\n\nWait, no: in sample input 1:\n\n- size 3: 3 slimes\n- size 5: 1\n- size 6: 1\n\nAfter merging two 3s: one 3, two 6s (one from merge, one original), one 5\nThen merge two 6s: one 3, one 5, one 12 \u2192 total 3.\n\nBut if we had not had the original 6, we would have only one 6 from merge, so no further merge.\n\nSo the presence of the original 6 caused an additional merge.\n\nSo **different initial sizes can interact** if their doubling chains meet.\n\nTherefore, we **cannot** process each size independently.\n\nWe must simulate the merging process globally, because a slime of size 6 can come from:\n- initial size 6\n- or from merging two size 3s\n\nSo we need to combine counts at the same size, even if they come from different sources.\n\nTherefore, the **heap + dictionary** simulation is correct.\n\nBut we must be careful: the count at a size can be very large, but the number of distinct sizes is not too big, because each merge doubles the size, so the depth is logarithmic.\n\nBut worst-case, we start with 1e5 distinct sizes, and each can generate O(log(max_value)) new sizes, so total distinct sizes is about 1e5 * log2(1e9) \u2248 1e5 * 30 = 3e6, which is acceptable? Maybe, but we must be careful.\n\nBut we can do better: we don\u2019t need to simulate each size individually if we process in sorted order and use a dictionary.\n\nAlternative efficient approach:\n\n- Use a dictionary `cnt`.\n- Get all initial keys, sort them.\n- But we may create new keys. So we can use a while loop that processes keys in sorted order, and whenever we add a new key, we remember to process it later.\n\nBut we can use a heap as described.\n\nLet\u2019s implement the heap approach.\n\nSteps:\n\n- Initialize `cnt` as dictionary.\n- Initialize min-heap with all initial sizes.\n- While heap not empty:\n  - pop smallest `s`\n  - if `cnt[s]` is 0, continue\n  - if `cnt[s] >= 2`:\n    - `carry = cnt[s] // 2`\n    - `rem = cnt[s] % 2`\n    - `cnt[s] = rem`\n    - `cnt[2*s] += carry`\n    - push `2*s` into heap\n  - (if `cnt[s] == 1`, we leave it and do nothing else)\n- After heap is empty, sum all `cnt[s]` for s in cnt.\n\nBut note: we might have pushed the same size multiple times, but that's okay.\n\nBut we must avoid infinite loop? No, because size doubles each time, and eventually we stop when count becomes 0 or 1.\n\nBut what if `carry` is 0? Then we don't push. But `carry = cnt[s]//2`, so if `cnt[s] < 2`, we don't do anything.\n\nSo we only push when `carry > 0`.\n\nBut we already pushed initial sizes.\n\nBut when we update `cnt[2*s]`, we must push `2*s` only if it's not already in the heap? But we can push it again \u2014 we\u2019ll just skip if we see it with count 0.\n\nBut to avoid too many duplicates, we can check if we are adding to a count that was zero, then push.\n\nBut for simplicity, we can push every time we update `cnt[2*s]`, even if it's already in the heap.\n\nBut worst-case, we might push many times.\n\nBut each size is generated at most O(log(max_value)) times? Not exactly, but each time we update a size, it's because a lower size merged into it.\n\nBut the total number of updates is bounded by the total number of merges, which is O(total_initial_slimes), which is too big.\n\nBut we are not simulating each merge, we are doing bulk: `cnt[s] // 2` is done in one step.\n\nSo the number of **operations** (heap pops) is at most the number of distinct sizes that ever have count > 0.\n\nAnd each initial size can generate O(log(max_size)) new sizes, so total distinct sizes is O(N * log(max_value)) \u2248 1e5 * 30 = 3e6, which is acceptable in Pyton? Maybe borderline.\n\nBut worst-case, we might have 3e6 heap operations, each O(log(3e6)) \u2248 20, so 60e6 operations, which is acceptable in Pyton in C++ but in Python might be borderline.\n\nBut note: the counts can be up to 1e9, but we are not iterating over each slime, we are doing integer division.\n\nSo the **per-operation** work is O(1), so total operations is the number of distinct size values we ever see.\n\nBut each initial size can generate a chain: s, 2s, 4s, ... until the count becomes 0 or 1.\n\nBut the count at each level is `carry` from below, so we can compute the entire chain without simulation?\n\nBut because different chains can merge (e.g., size 3 and size 6 can both contribute to size 6), we cannot process chains independently.\n\nSo we must use the global simulation.\n\nBut worst-case, the number of distinct sizes might be large.\n\nAlternative: use a dictionary and process keys in sorted order repeatedly until no more changes? But we can do:\n\n- Use a while loop that processes all current keys in sorted order, and if we add new keys, we do another pass.\n\nBut same issue.\n\nBut note: the sizes grow exponentially, so for each initial size, the chain is at most O(log(max_value)) long.\n\nBut when chains merge, we combine counts.\n\nSo we can do:\n\n- Use a dictionary `cnt`.\n- Use a list of keys, sort it.\n- For each key in sorted order:\n  - if `cnt[key] >= 2`:\n    - `carry = cnt[key] // 2`\n    - `rem = cnt[key] % 2`\n    - `cnt[key] = rem`\n    - `cnt[2*key] += carry`\n    - (and remember that 2*key might be smaller than some keys? no, 2*key > key, so if we process in increasing order, we haven't processed 2*key yet? but we might have already processed keys between key and 2*key)\n\nExample: keys: [3,5,6]\nWe process 3: creates 6 \u2192 now cnt[6] becomes 2 (if was 1)\nThen when we process 5: no change\nThen when we process 6: we see cnt[6]=2, so we merge to 12.\n\nBut if we process in sorted order, we process 3, then 5, then 6, so we do see the updated 6.\n\nBut what if we have size 12 already? Then we process 12 later.\n\nBut if we do one pass in sorted order, we might miss that 2*key might be smaller than some keys we already processed? No, because 2*key > key, and we process in increasing order, so we haven't processed 2*key yet.\n\nBut what if 2*key is less than some key we already processed? No, because we process in increasing order, and 2*key > key, so if we process keys in sorted order, we process key before 2*key.\n\nBut 2*key might be less than some keys we haven't processed? Yes, but that's fine \u2014 we will process it when we get to it.\n\nBut what if 2*key is smaller than some key that we already processed? Only if 2*key < some key that is greater than key, but since we process in increasing order, we process key, then later keys, so 2*key might be greater than some keys we haven't processed, but we will get to it.\n\nBut what if 2*key is less than a key that we already processed? That would happen only if 2*key < key, which is impossible.\n\nSo 2*key > key, so if we process in increasing order, we will process key before 2*key.\n\nBut what if 2*key is less than a key that comes after key in sorted order? Yes, but we will process it later.\n\nBut the problem: when we update `cnt[2*key]`, we might be increasing it, but we haven't processed 2*key yet, so when we get to it, we will see the updated count.\n\nSo one pass in sorted order is sufficient? No, because:\n\n- We process key A, which produces carry to 2A.\n- Then we process key B > A, which might produce carry to 2B.\n- Then we process 2A, which might produce carry to 4A.\n- But 4A might be less than some keys we already processed? No, 4A > 2A > A, and we process in increasing order, so we haven't processed 4A yet.\n\nBut what if 4A is less than some key that we already processed? Only if 4A < some key < 2A, but that's impossible because 4A > 2A.\n\nSo the chain is increasing.\n\nTherefore, if we process all keys in sorted order once, and whenever we add a new key (2*key), we insert it in sorted order, but we can do multiple passes.\n\nBut to be safe, we can do:\n\n- Use a dictionary.\n- Use a sorted list of keys.\n- But we are adding new keys.\n\nAlternatively, we can use a heap, as planned.\n\nLet\u2019s implement the heap version.\n\nBut we must avoid infinite loop and duplicate pushes.\n\nWe\u2019ll do:\n\n```python\nimport heapq\n\nN = int(input())\ncnt = {}\nheap = []\n\nfor _ in range(N):\n    s, c = map(int, input().split())\n    cnt[s] = cnt.get(s, 0) + c\n    heapq.heappush(heap, s)\n\n# We'll process until heap is empty\nwhile heap:\n    s = heapq.heappop(heap)\n    if cnt[s] == 0:\n        continue\n    if cnt[s] >= 2:\n        carry = cnt[s] // 2\n        rem = cnt[s] % 2\n        cnt[s] = rem\n        # Add carry to 2*s\n        double_s = 2 * s\n        cnt[double_s] = cnt.get(double_s, 0) + carry\n        heapq.heappush(heap, double_s)\n    # if cnt[s] == 1, we leave it and do nothing else\n\n# Now sum all remaining counts\ntotal = sum(cnt.values())\nprint(total)\n```\n\nBut wait: what if `cnt[s]` becomes 0? We set it to `rem`, which is 0 or 1.\n\nBut when we do `cnt[s] = rem`, and if rem==0, then later we skip.\n\nBut we might have pushed `s` multiple times? But we only push initial sizes and when we create a new size.\n\nBut when we pop a size, we process it only if count > 0.\n\nBut what if a size is pushed multiple times? That's okay.\n\nLet\u2019s test with sample 1:\n\nInitial: cnt = {3:3,5:1,6:1}, heap=[3,5,6] (min-heap)\n\nPop s=3, cnt[3]=3 >=2 -> carry=1, rem=1 -> cnt[3]=1, cnt[6]=1+1=2, push 6 -> heap=[5,6,6]\n\nPop s=5, cnt[5]=1 -> skip (do nothing, and we don't push anything)\n\nPop s=6, cnt[6]=2 >=2 -> carry=1, rem=0 -> cnt[6]=0, cnt[12]=0+1=1, push 12 -> heap=[6,12]\n\nPop s=6, cnt[6]=0 -> skip\n\nPop s=12, cnt[12]=1 -> do nothing\n\nTotal = cnt[3]+cnt[5]+cnt[6]+cnt[12] = 1+1+0+1 = 3 -> correct.\n\nSample 2: all counts=1, so no merge -> total=3.\n\nSample 3: s=1000000000, c=1000000000\n\nWe will repeatedly merge:\n\n- cnt[1e9] = 1e9 -> carry = 5e8, rem = 0 -> cnt[1e9]=0, cnt[2e9] += 5e8, push 2e9\n- then pop 2e9: cnt=5e8 -> carry=2.5e8, rem=0 -> cnt[4e9] += 2.5e8\n- then 4e9: 2.5e8 -> carry=1.25e8, rem=0\n- ... until carry becomes 0.\n\nBut eventually, we keep dividing by 2 until the count becomes odd.\n\nThe number of times we can divide 1e9 by 2 until it becomes 0? But we are doing integer division.\n\nBut the final total slimes is the number of ones in the binary representation of 1e9.\n\nBut wait: we are not just counting the popcount, because we are doing:\n\n- Each time we take a count c at size s, we set cnt[s] = c % 2, and add c//2 to 2s.\n\nSo the total number of slimes is the sum of (c mod 2) over all sizes.\n\nBut this is exactly the **total number of ones in the binary representations of all the counts in the entire merging tree**? No.\n\nActually, it's the sum of the remainders at each size.\n\nBut because of merging across chains, it's not simply the sum of popcount of initial counts.\n\nBut in sample 3, only one chain, so it should be popcount(1000000000).\n\nLet me compute popcount of 1000000000.\n\n1000000000 in binary:\n\nWe know 2^30 \u2248 1e9.\n\n1000000000 = 10^9\n\n2^30 = 1073741824\n2^29 = 536870912\n\n10^9 - 536870912 = 463129088\n2^28 = 268435456 -> 463129088 - 268435456 = 194693632\n2^27 = 134217728 -> 194693632 - 134217728 = 60475904\n2^26 = 67108864 > 60475904, so no\n2^25 = 33554432 -> 60475904 - 33554432 = 26921472\n2^24 = 16777216 -> 26921472 - 16777216 = 10144256\n2^23 = 8388608 -> 10144256 - 8388608 = 1755648\n2^21 = 2097152 > 1755648\n2^20 = 1048576 -> 1755648 - 1048576 = 707072\n2^19 = 524288 -> 707072 - 524288 = 182784\n2^17 = 131072 -> 182784 - 131072 = 51712\n2^15 = 32768 -> 51712 - 32768 = 18944\n2^14 = 16384 -> 18944 - 16384 = 2560\n2^11 = 2048 -> 2560 - 2048 = 512\n2^9 = 512 -> 512 - 512 = 0\n\nSo the exponents: 29,28,27,25,24,23,20,19,17,15,14,11,9\n\nCount: 13 ones.\n\nSo output should be 13.\n\nOur simulation should give 13.\n\nBut in the simulation, we are not summing the popcount, we are simulating the merges.\n\nBut for a single chain, it should be the popcount.\n\nSo the code should work.\n\nBut in the heap simulation, we are pushing new sizes, and the total number of operations is the number of distinct sizes we create, which is about 30 for this chain.\n\nSo it's efficient.\n\nLet\u2019s code accordingly.\n\nBut note: we might have multiple initial sizes that eventually map to the same doubling chain.\n\nBut the simulation handles that.\n\nOne more test: two sizes that merge into same chain.\n\nExample: \n2\n2 3\n4 1\n\n- size 2:3, size 4:1\n- process 2: carry=1, rem=1 -> cnt[2]=1, cnt[4]=1+1=2\n- process 4: carry=1, rem=0 -> cnt[4]=0, cnt[8]=1\n- total: cnt[2]=1, cnt[8]=1 -> total=2\n\nBinary: 3 has popcount=2, 1 has popcount=1, but they merged at 4, so not independent.\n\nBut the simulation gives 2.\n\nIs that minimal? \n- Start: three 2s, one 4\n- merge two 2s -> one 4, now: one 2, two 4s\n- merge two 4s -> one 8\n- result: one 2, one 8 -> total 2.\n\nYes.\n\nSo code:\n\n```python\nimport heapq\n\nN = int(input())\ncnt = {}\nheap = []\n\nfor _ in range(N):\n    s, c = map(int, input().split())\n    cnt[s] = cnt.get(s, 0) + c\n    heapq.heappush(heap, s)\n\nwhile heap:\n    s = heapq.heappop(heap)\n    if cnt[s] == 0:\n        continue\n    if cnt[s] >= 2:\n        carry = cnt[s] // 2\n        rem = cnt[s] % 2\n        cnt[s] = rem\n        double_s = 2 * s\n        cnt[double_s] = cnt.get(double_s, 0) + carry\n        heapq.heappush(heap, double_s)\n\ntotal = sum(cnt.values())\nprint(total)\n```\n\nLet\u2019s run sample 3: should output 13.\n\nBut note: the heap may have many duplicates, but it's okay.\n\nWe'll submit.\n\nBut note: we are not removing old entries from heap, but we skip if cnt[s]==0.\n\nThis is standard lazy heap.\n\nI