` in the total sum.\n\nAh! This is crucial.\n\nWe are forming a **sum** of a subsequence. So:\n\n- To get a contribution of `2^i` in the sum, we can use:\n  - one `2^i`, or\n  - two `2^(i-1)`, or\n  - four `2^(i-2)`, etc.\n\nSo we don't need an actual `2^i` \u2014 we can use smaller ones.\n\nSo we should use a **carry-like** approach:\n\nWe process from bit 0 to 31:\n\n- Let `have` = freq[i] + carry_from_lower  (but carry from lower doesn't make sense)\n\nWait \u2014 better: we can use a **greedy accumulation** from low to high:\n\nIdea from known solutions to similar problems:\n\n- Use an array `cnt` for exponents.\n- Start from bit 0 to 31.\n- Let `need = target` and process bits.\n- But instead, we can simulate: we need to cover `target` using available powers, and we can break larger ones.\n\nBut here's a known efficient solution:\n\nWe can use a variable `cur` representing how many numbers we have at current bit level, and process from low to high.\n\nBut let me refer to a known pattern:\n\nWe can do:\n\n```python\ncnt = [0]*32\nfor x in nums:\n    cnt[x.bit_length()-1] += 1\n\nops = 0\nfor i in range(32):\n    if target >> i & 1:\n        if cnt[i] > 0:\n            cnt[i] -= 1\n        else:\n            # we need to get a 2^i by splitting a higher number\n            # find next available higher bit\n            j = i + 1\n            while j < 32 and cnt[j] == 0:\n                j += 1\n            if j == 32:\n                return -1\n            # now split one 2^j down to 2^i\n            for k in range(j, i, -1):\n                cnt[k] -= 1\n                cnt[k-1] += 2\n                ops += 1\n            # now we have at least two at level i, use one\n            cnt[i] -= 1\n    # carry over unused cnt[i] to the next level? No \u2014 we can't merge.\n    # but we can use two 2^i to simulate one 2^(i+1) in terms of value? \n    # But no \u2014 we can't merge, but for the purpose of forming higher bits in target, \n    # we don't need to merge \