ut I need to be careful about overlapping domains.\n\nBetter approach: process values in ascending order and use union-find or something, but that's complex.\n\nSimple O(n\u00b2) might work for small inputs but not 10^5.\n\nLet me think of a stack-based approach.\n\nIdea: use monotonic stack to find for each element, the next greater element. Then for each value, I can determine the regions where it can be maximum.\n\nActually, here's a better approach:\n1. For each value v, find all positions where nums[i] = v\n2. For each such position, find the left and right boundaries where v is the maximum (using next greater element concept)\n3. But this is tricky because multiple vs.\n\nI found a simpler insight: a subarray [i,j] is valid if and only if nums[i] = nums[j] = max(nums[i:j+1]).\n\nI can iterate through all starting positions, and for each, extend while keeping track of maximum, and check when current element equals the maximum and the starting element equals the maximum. But this is O(n\u00b2).\n\nFor 10^5, O(n\u00b2) is 10^10, which is too slow in Python.\n\nI need a more efficient solution.\n\nLet me search for patterns. \n\nAnother idea: process elements in descending order of value. For each value v, consider all positions with value v. In the array, temporarily treat all elements > v as barriers. Then in each contiguous segment separated by barriers, if there are k positions with value v, then the number of valid subarrays with maximum v is k*(k+1)/2 (all pairs of positions with v in the same segment).\n\nLet me test this:\nFor [1,4,3,3,2]:\n- v=4: positions [1], barriers: none (since no >4), segment: entire array, k=1, count=1\n- v=3: positions [2,3], barriers: element 4 at pos 1, so segments: [0,1] and [2,4]. In [0,1]: no 3s. In [2,4]: positions 2,3 have 3, so k=2, count=2*3/2=3\n- v=2: positions [4], in segment [4,4] (since 4>2 is barrier at pos 1, 3>2 at pos 2,3), so segment [4,4], k=1, count=1\n- v=1: positions [0], segment [0,0] (barriers at pos 1,2,3,4), k=1, count=1\nTotal: 1+3+1+1 = 6. Correct!\n\nFor [3,3,3]:\n- v=3: positions [0,1,2], no barriers (no >3), one segment [0,2], k=3, count=3*4/2=6. Correct!\n\nFor [1]:\n- v=1: positions [0], k=1, count=1. Correct!\n\nPerfect! The algorithm:\n1. Group positions by value, sort values in descending order\n2. Maintain a data structure of current \"gaps\" or segments\n3. For each value v from high to low:\n   a. Find all positions with nums[i] = v\n   b. The barriers are positions with values > v (already processed)\n   c. The current segments are the gaps between barriers\n   d. For each segment, count how many v-positions are in it, say k\n   e. Add k*(k+1)/2 to result\n4. Return result\n\nTo implement the segments, I can use a sorted list of barrier positions, and for each value v, the segments are the intervals between consecutive barriers.\n\nActually, since we process in descending order, when processing v, all positions with value > v are already barriers.\n\nI can maintain a sorted list of all barrier positions (positions with values > v).\n\nFor each value v, I need to:\n- Get all positions i where nums[i] = v\n- Find which segments these positions fall into, where segments are defined by the barrier positions\n- For each segment, count the number of v-positions in it\n- Add combinations for each segment\n\nTo find segments, I can sort the barrier positions, then the segments are:\n- From 0 to first barrier\n- Between consecutive barriers\n- From last barrier to n-1\n\nBut I need to be careful about the positions.\n\nActually, the segments are the maximal contiguous intervals that don't contain any barrier.\n\nSince barriers are positions with values > v, and we're processing v, barriers are all positions processed so far with higher values.\n\nI can maintain a sorted list of barrier positions.\n\nFor each value v:\n1. Get list P of positions where nums[i] = v\n2. If no barriers, then all positions in P are in one segment\n3. Else, use binary search to find 