y value\n2. Sort values in descending order\n3. Initialize barriers as empty sorted list\n4. For each value in sorted order:\n   a. Get positions P for this value, sort them\n   b. Group P into segments based on barriers\n   c. For each group of size k, add k*(k+1)/2\n   d. Add all positions in P to barriers\n5. Return total\n\nTo maintain barriers as sorted list, I can use a list and keep it sorted, or use bisect.\n\nI'll use bisect to maintain sorted order.\n\nFor grouping: sort P, then iterate through P, and for each consecutive pair, check if there's a barrier between them.\n\nTo check if there's a barrier in (p1, p2), I can:\n- Find the leftmost barrier > p1\n- If it exists and < p2, then there is a barrier\n\nUsing bisect_left on barriers.\n\n```python\nimport bisect\n\ndef countSubarrays(nums):\n    n = len(nums)\n    \n    # Group positions by value\n    val_to_pos = {}\n    for i, val in enumerate(nums):\n        if val not in val_to_pos:\n            val_to_pos[val] = []\n        val_to_pos[val].append(i)\n    \n    # Sort values in descending order\n    values = sorted(val_to_pos.keys(), reverse=True)\n    \n    barriers = []  # sorted list of positions with values > current v\n    total = 0\n    \n    for val in values:\n        P = sorted(val_to_pos[val])  # positions with this value\n        if not P:\n            continue\n            \n        # Group positions in P based on barriers\n        groups = []\n        if P:\n            current_group = [P[0]]\n            for i in range(1, len(P)):\n                prev_pos = P[i-1]\n                curr_pos = P[i]\n                # Check if there is any barrier in (prev_pos, curr_pos)\n                # Find first barrier > prev_pos\n                idx = bisect.bisect_right(barriers, prev_pos)\n                if idx < len(barriers) and barriers[idx] < curr_pos:\n                    # There is a barrier between prev_pos and curr_pos\n                    groups.append(current_group)\n                   