\nC. Questionable Cause\nD. Fallacy of Composition\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{B}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 440, "output_tokens": 245, "total_tokens": 685}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_philosophy_9542", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The Dean was showing a visitor around the campus. The visitor asked, \"About how many students do you have here?\" After a moment of reflection the Dean replied, \"Oh, I'd guess about one in a hundred.\"\n\nOptions: \nA. Appeal to Authority\nB. Red Herring\nC. Denying the Antecedent\nD. False Dilemma\nE. Slippery Slope\nF. Equivocation\nG. Straw Man\nH. Fallacy of Division\nI. Circular Reasoning\nJ. Ad Hominem\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{F}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 708, "output_tokens": 381, "total_tokens": 1089}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_philosophy_9637", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: E.F. Schumacher, famous economist, in an article in the Atlantic (April 1979): Fifteen months [after I had advised that rural India should have a technology intermediate between the hoe and the tractor,] an all-India conference on intermediate technology was arranged. (They still didn't like the term [intermediate], so they called it appropriate technology. That's all right: when I come to India and somebody says, \"Oh, Mr. Schumacher, I don't believe in appropriate technology\", I just look him straight in the face and say, \"Oh, that's splendid. Do you believe in inappropriate technology?\")\n\nOptions: \nA. Equivocation\nB. Inconsistency\nC. Appeal to Authority\nD. Prejudicial Language\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{A}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 970, "output_tokens": 491, "total_tokens": 1461}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_philosophy_9961", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion:  Lukianoff and Haidt argue that the disinvitation of certain speakers\n\nOptions: \nA. is a form of cognitive distortion.\nB. will eventually lead to a decline in student enrollment.\nC. will encourage students to disregard positive aspects of a person's history.\nD. will lead to a decline in university reputation.\nE. will only serve to promote intellectual homogeneity on college and universities.\nF. All of the above.\nG. is an example of mental filtering.\nH. is a violation of the speaker's freedom of speech.\nI. is a proactive measure to safeguard student's mental health.\nJ. will foster a culture of intolerance and narrow-mindedness on campuses.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{J}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 922, "output_tokens": 435, "total_tokens": 1357}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_philosophy_9786", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion:  Against Kant's view of sexual objectification, Papadaki argues that\n\nOptions: \nA. Papadaki actually agrees with Kant's view\nB. Papadaki suggests that Kant's view of sexual objectification is too subjective.\nC. Papadaki argues that sexual objectification is an outdated concept.\nD. sexual objectification actually enhances humanity.\nE. Papadaki believes that sexual objectification is a necessary part of human relationships.\nF. Papadaki argues that Kant's view of sexual objectification is too moralistic.\nG. According to Papadaki, Kant's views on sexual objectification are too black and white.\nH. Papadaki holds that sexual objectification can be justified in certain contexts.\nI. Kant is wrong to think about the wrong of sexual objectification in terms of humanity.\nJ. it is not clear how sexual use sacrifices humanity.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{H}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 968, "output_tokens": 317, "total_tokens": 1285}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_philosophy_9870", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: At the end of the course, Professor Jones told us that we were the best class he had ever had. That's why I'm sure that the 0.0 the appeared on my grade report is a mistake. Surely, if we were as good a class as Professor Jones said we were, none of us deserved to fail.\n\nOptions: \nA. Appeal to Gallery (Provicialism)\nB. Fallacy of Composition\nC. Red Herring\nD. Appeal to Authority\nE. Straw Man\nF. Fallacy of Division\nG. Slippery Slope\nH. Appeal to Ignorance\nI. False Dilemma\nJ. Questionable Cause\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{B}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 808, "output_tokens": 349, "total_tokens": 1157}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_philosophy_9591", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: William Safire in American Heritage Magazine (December 1987): ...The greatness of Lincoln was his purposefulness. He saw something that few others saw: that the democratic experiment centered on the Union and that majority rule was everything. The minute you allowed secession, you made democracy an absurdity---because what would happen would be continued subdivision.\n\nOptions: \nA. Questionable Analogy\nB. Red Herring\nC. Appeal to Ignorance\nD. Appeal to Authority\nE. Ad Hominem\nF. Slippery Slope\nG. Circular Argument\nH. False Dilemma\nI. Straw Man\nJ. Hasty Generalization\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{F}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 865, "output_tokens": 305, "total_tokens": 1170}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_philosophy_9972", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Tan ah Tiat, forty-nine years old, a native of Kuala Lumpar, Malaysia, was charged with possession of opium. Arguing for acquittal, he told the judge that his opium habit did no harm, as he was too old to work anyway. Asked how he lived, he replied that he lived on the earnings of his grandmother.\n\nOptions: \nA. Straw Man\nB. False Dilemma\nC. Inconsistency\nD. Appeal to Authority\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{C}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 666, "output_tokens": 336, "total_tokens": 1002}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_philosophy_9872", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Men are better drivers than women are. The proof of this is that men are more capable than women of managing cars on the road.\n\nOptions: \nA. False Dilemma\nB. Appeal to Pity\nC. Begging the question\nD. Appeal to Authority\nE. Bandwagon Fallacy\nF. Ad hominem (Attacking the Person)\nG. Appeal to 