 professionals have adequate training and certification.\nF. guaranteeing that the needs of patients belonging to a particular group or population are being met.\nG. improving the health status and satisfaction of patients.\nH. preventing the spread of disease and infection within the health care setting.\nI. ensuring that all health care facilities are up to code.\nJ. reducing program costs and beneficiary expenditures.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{G}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 824, "output_tokens": 224, "total_tokens": 1048}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_psychology_2361", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The driver of a car that has pulled up next to you at a red light turns up the volume on her radio. The increase in perceived sound that you experience is primarily due to\n\nOptions: \nA. increased secretions by the pituitary gland\nB. changes in the atmospheric pressure\nC. changes in the temperature of the air\nD. neurons firing more intensely\nE. a decrease in the distance between the sound source and your ear\nF. increased frequency of the radio waves\nG. increase in the speed of sound\nH. changing frequency of sound waves\nI. vibrations of the car windows\nJ. more neurons firing more frequently\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{J}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 848, "output_tokens": 271, "total_tokens": 1119}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_psychology_2569", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Define culture. What does Benedict (1934) mean by cultural relativity?\n\nOptions: \nA. Cultural relativity is the practice of comparing different cultural practices without bias.\nB. Cultural relativity is the idea that one's own culture is superior to others.\nC. Cultural relativity is the economic system through which cultural goods are produced and distributed.\nD. Cultural relativity is a theory that suggests culture is inherited genetically.\nE. Cultural relativity is the study of cultural differences.\nF. Cultural relativity is the belief that human behavior can only be understood and evaluated within the context of its cultural environment.\nG. Cultural relativity is the adaptation of culture to its environment.\nH. Cultural relativity is the scientific study of how culture is transmitted through language and symbols.\nI. Cultural relativity is the belief that all cultures are equal.\nJ. Cultural relativity is the process by which a culture adopts and assimilates foreign cultural elements.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{F}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1168, "output_tokens": 301, "total_tokens": 1469}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_psychology_2400", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The primary goal of a newly-developed community-based mental health program is to help people recently released from a psychiatric hospital adjust to life in the community. This is an example of:\n\nOptions: \nA. early intervention.\nB. reactive intervention.\nC. quaternary prevention.\nD. emergency intervention.\nE. crisis intervention.\nF. tertiary prevention.\nG. primary prevention.\nH. preventive care.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{F}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 609, "output_tokens": 260, "total_tokens": 869}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_psychology_1886", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What are standard scores and percentiles and why are they important?\n\nOptions: \nA. Standard scores and percentiles are used to find the mean score.\nB. Standard scores and percentiles help the examiner compare different individuals on the same test and the same individual on different tests.\nC. Standard scores provide a pass or fail status, whereas percentiles give a detailed performance analysis.\nD. Percentiles indicate the exact number of questions answered correctly, while standard scores represent the total test time.\nE. Standard scores are derived from the test-taker's age, while percentiles compare scores across different age groups.\nF. Standard scores are used to determine the difficulty level of the test, while percentiles rank the test takers.\nG. Standard scores and percentiles are not important in testing.\nH. Percentiles show the percentage of the test covered, while standard scores indicate the passing mark.\nI. Standard scores and percentiles are used to adjust the scores for varying test versions.\nJ. Standard scores and percentiles are used to calculate the raw score.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{B}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1177, "output_tokens": 376, "total_tokens": 1553}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_psychology_2429", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Discuss and distinguish between discrete and continuous values.\n\nOptions: \nA. Continuous values can take on any fractional or integer value between specified limits, while discrete values are usually restricted to whole-number values.\nB. Continuous values are countable in finite steps, while discrete values can represent an infinite range.\nC. Continuous values are restricted to a specific set of numbers, while discrete values can be any number within a range.\nD. Continuous values are always integers, while discrete values can be fractions.\nE. Discrete values have a clear distinction between each other, while continuous values overlap.\nF. Discrete values are used for categorization, while continuous values cannot be categorized.\nG. Continuous values are only applicable in digital systems, while discrete values are used in analog systems.\nH. Discrete values can only be whole numbers, while continuous values can only be fractions.\nI. Discrete values are measured in intervals, while continuous values are not measurable.\nJ. Discrete values can take any value, while continuous values are restricted to whole numbers.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{A}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1352, "output_tokens": 306, "total_tokens": 1658}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_psychology_2466", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Asking an obese person to restrict eating to limited situations, such as a specific place in the kitchen at particular predetermined times of day, is best described as an example of\n\nOptions: \nA. counter conditioning\nB. response control\nC. exposure therapy\nD. cognitive behavioral therapy\nE. aversive conditioning\nF. seif-punishment\nG. self-monitoring\nH. reinforcement control\nI. systematic desensitization\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{B}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 691, "output_tokens": 285, "total_tokens": 976}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_psychology_2609", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What is an EPSP? What is an IPSP? How do they affect a nerve cell's excitability?\n\nOptions: \nA. EPSP and IPSP are types of nerve cells.\nB. Both EPSP and IPSP reduce the likelihood of a nerve cell reaching threshold and firing an action potential.\nC. EPSP and IPSP are neurotransmitters released by glial cells that do not directly affect nerve cells.\nD. EPSP refers to a nerve cell's energy production surge, while IPSP refers to its energy conservation state.\nE. EPSP is an inhibitory postsynaptic potential and IPSP is an excitatory postsynaptic potential.\nF. An EPSP is a permanent change in nerve cell excitability, while an IPSP is a temporary change that lasts only a few seconds.\nG. An EPSP is an excitatory postsynaptic potential making a nerve cell more likely to fire and an IPSP is an inhibitory post synaptic potential making it less likely to fire.\nH. EPSP is a protein that enhances nerve growth, and IPSP is a molecule that prevents nerve growth.\nI. EPSP increases a nerve cell's excitability by decreasing its membrane potential, while IPSP decreases excitability by increasing the membrane potential.\nJ. EPSP and IPSP do not affect a nerve cell's excitability.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{G}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1412, "output_tokens": 358, "total_tokens": 1770}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_psychology_2000", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Kohler's studies with apes are seen as demonstrating\n\nOptions: \nA. that apes are able to use objects from their natural environment as tools.\nB. that apes can learn through operant conditioning.\nC. that learning can occur via insight.\nD. the apes' ability to communicate.\nE. latent learning in primates.\nF. the apes' ability to recognize themselves in a mirror.\nG. that apes are capable of abstract thinking.\nH. the apes' ability to mimic human behavior.\nI. the apes' ability to solve complex mathematical problems.\nJ. that apes have a basic understanding of human languages.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{C}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 813, "output_tokens": 297, "total_tokens": 1110}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_psychology_1899", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Discuss the condition known as \"masking.\" How does this phenomenoninfluence the sound produced by an orchestra ?\n\nOptions: \nA. Masking refers to the physical masks worn by musicians.\nB. Masking refers to the use of physical barriers between different sections of the orchestra to prevent sound interference.\nC. Masking is a phenomenon where the presence of one sound makes another difficult to hear. In an orchestra, lower frequency instruments mask the sounds of higher-frequency ones, musicians compensate for this when playing together.\nD. Masking occurs when the conductor's movements distract the audience from the actual sounds of the orchestra.\nE. Masking is the process by which string instruments dominate wind instruments, making them inaudible, regardless of the actual volume.\nF. Masking is a psychological effect where listeners perceive a change in the tempo of the music due to the acoustics of the concert hall.\nG. Masking is a phenomenon where the presence of one color makes another difficult to see.\nH. Masking is a lighting technique in orchestra.\nI. Masking is when musicians adjust their playing volume to blend with other instruments, but it does not affect the frequency of sounds produced.\nJ. Masking is the intentional muting of certain instruments by the composer to highlight others, not related to the acoustic phenomenon of sound interference.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{C}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1593, "output_tokens": 366, "total_tokens": 1959}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_psychology_1954", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Describe the use of narcotics and the common treatment for theirabuse.\n\nOptions: \nA. Narcotics are used to treat anxiety and their abuse is managed through meditation and relaxation techniques.\nB. Narcotics are used to induce sleep and their abuse is treated with sleep therapy\nC. Narcotics are primarily utilized for weight loss and their abuse is commonly treated with dietary adjustments.\nD. Narcotics are used for enhancing performance and their abuse is treated with physical therapy\nE. Narcotics are used for their anticoagulant effects and their abuse is treated with blood thinners adjustments.\nF. Narcotics are administered for cognitive enhancement and their abuse is treated with cognitive behavioral therapy.\nG. Narcotics are used for recreational purposes and their abuse is treated with counselling\nH. Narcotics are used for their anti-inflammatory properties and abuse is treated with alternative herbal remedies.\nI. Narcotics are used to manage chronic fatigue and their abuse is treated with energy-boosting supplements.\nJ. The use of narcotics involves controlling pain and their common treatment for abuse includes combating the physiological addiction with drugs like methadone and combining this with social factors like live-in resident facilities.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{J}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1284, "output_tokens": 349, "total_tokens": 1633}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_psychology_2497", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Large deficits of water can be only partly compensated for by renal conservation; drinking is the ultimate compensatory mechanism. What stimulates the subjective feeling of thirst which drives one to drink water?\n\nOptions: \nA. Decreased blood sodium levels\nB. Low plasma osmolarity\nC. Low body temperature\nD. High blood pressure\nE. Low levels of antidiuretic hormone (ADH)\nF. A high intracellular fluid volume\nG. A low extracellular fluid volume and a high plasma osmolarity\nH. High extracellular fluid volume\nI. Increased saliva production\nJ. High glucose levels in the blood\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "