committed atrocities did so only when under direct threat to their own lives\nJ. Only those with a history of criminal behavior were capable of committing the atrocities\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{G}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1224, "output_tokens": 423, "total_tokens": 1647}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_psychology_2565", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What is proactive interference and what does it indicate about how information is classified? What is retroactive interference?\n\nOptions: \nA. Retroactive interference is when past memories hinder the learning of new information, while proactive interference is when new information enhances the recall of old memories.\nB. Retroactive interference is the effect of new information on the recall of previously learned information\nC. Proactive interference is the enhancement of future memory retention due to past information, while retroactive interference is the inability to forget unimportant details.\nD. Retroactive interference is the process of past memories aiding the retention of new information, while proactive interference is when new learning disrupts the recall of future information.\nE. Proactive interference refers to the inability to recall new information\nF. Proactive interference is the effect of prior learned items on the retention of information, indicating the importance of classification in retention. Retroactive interference refers to the effect the last items learned have on the retention of information.\nG. Proactive interference is the inability to learn new information because of existing knowledge, while retroactive interference is the improvement of memory retention due to new information.\nH. Proactive interference occurs when the memorization of new information is facilitated by previously learned information, and retroactive interference is the process where past information is confused with new learning.\nI. Proactive interference is the process by which future information is predicted based on past memories, and retroactive interference is when new information overwrites old memories.\nJ. Proactive interference is the effect of future learned items on the retention of information\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{F}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1980, "output_tokens": 474, "total_tokens": 2454}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_psychology_2428", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: If you rub your eyes long and hard enough, you would eventuallysee some type of visual pattern. Explain this phenomenon.\n\nOptions: \nA. The rubbing action generates light inside the eye\nB. Pressure on the eye temporarily alters the shape of the cornea, leading to distorted visual signals.\nC. The mechanical stimulation from rubbing triggers a reflex that causes the pupils to dilate and create patterned visuals.\nD. Friction from rubbing generates heat, which causes the illusion of light patterns.\nE. It happens due to the production of a natural hallucinogen when pressure is applied\nF. The pressure causes the release of stored light energy in the eye\nG. The pressure exerted on the eye stimulates the receptor nerve cells at the back of the retina, which normally respond to light, and their response to this pressure, when communicated to the visual area of the brain, produce the experience of light.\nH. Rubbing the eyes causes a temporary change in blood flow to the visual cortex, resulting in visual phenomena.\nI. The rubbing motion activates latent photoreceptor cells which produce the visual patterns.\nJ. The optic nerve is temporarily disoriented by the rubbing, resulting in random visual patterns.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{G}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1372, "output_tokens": 371, "total_tokens": 1743}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_psychology_2005", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Because Jake, the team's starting, star center, is late to basketball practice, Coach Peterson sits him out for the first quarter of the next game. Jake is on time for the rest of the season. Coach Peterson has used\n\nOptions: \nA. modeling.\nB. shaping.\nC. learned helplessness.\nD. positive reinforcement.\nE. positive punishment.\nF. negative reinforcement.\nG. omission training.\nH. extinction.\nI. counterconditioning.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{E}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 628, "output_tokens": 232, "total_tokens": 860}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_psychology_2362", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Every time you buy ice cream from the Yellow Brick Road ice cream parlor, you get your over-the-rainbow card stamped. Once you purchase ten items, you get your next item free. The Yellow Brick Road ice cream parlor has you on which of the following reinforcement schedules?\n\nOptions: \nA. Variable-interval\nB. Fixed-interval\nC. Continuous reinforcement\nD. Variable-ratio with extinction\nE. Fixed-ratio\nF. Variable-ratio\nG. Interval-ratio\nH. Partial reinforcement\nI. Ratio reinforcement\nJ. Fixed-variable\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{E}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 794, "output_tokens": 287, "total_tokens": 1081}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_psychology_2281", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: You are subpoenaed to appear at a deposition to testify about a former client of yours. During the deposition, the attorney for the opposing party asks you to provide confidential client information. You should:\n\nOptions: \nA. provide the information only if it puts the former client in a favorable light.\nB. provide the information if the 