% on over $8,000 up to $10,000, 14% on over $10,000 up to $12,000, 16% on over $12,000.\n\nSo for $12,000:\n- over $6,000: $6,000, so:\n- $2,000 at 10% = $200\n- $2,000 at 12% = $240\n- $2,000 at 14% = $280\n- total surtax = $720\n\nSo total tax = $840 + $720 = $1,560.\n\nFor $15,100:\nNormal tax:\n- up to $12,000: $840\n- $12,000 to $14,000: $2,000 at 14% = $280\n- $14,000 to $15,100: $1,100 at 16% = $176\n- normal tax = $840 + $280 + $176 = $1,296\n\nSurtax:\n- over $6,000: $9,100\n- $2,000 at 10% = $200\n- $2,000 at 12% = $240\n- $2,000 at 14% = $280\n- $3,100 at 16% = $496\n- surtax = $200+240+280+496 = $1,216\n\nTotal tax = $1,296 + $1,216 = $2,512\n\nSo for $12,000 income: tax = $1,560\nFor $15,100: tax = $2,512\nAdditional tax = $2,512 - $1,560 = $952\n\nSo per shareholder, additional tax on $3,100 dividend = $952\nSo after-tax increase in income = $3,100 - $952 = $2,148\n\nFor two shareholders: $4,296\n\nNot in options.\n\nBut $2,148 is close to $2,250.\n\nPerhaps for $15,100, the brackets are different.\n\nMaybe the corporate tax is calculated differently.\n\nPerhaps the $30,000 is taxed at a lower rate.\n\nAnother idea: perhaps the additional $10,000 is taxed at the corporate level at 38%, so $3,800, leaving $6,200.\n\nThen this $6,200 is distributed, and the individual tax on the additional income.\n\nBut perhaps the answer is $2,250, as the closest.\n\nOr perhaps they want the increase per shareholder, and $2,250 is an option.\n\nBut the question says \"stockholders' income\" \u2014 plural.\n\nPerhaps the increase is $2,250 in total.\n\nLet's see option D is $2,250", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1396, "output_tokens": 24000, "total_tokens": 25396}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_business_439", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer b