 business practices.\nE. They favored the principles of socialism.\nF. They believed in the laws of supply and demand.\nG. They were satisfied with the state of the economy.\nH. They believed laissez-faire policies would prevent economic crises.\nI. They felt that laissez-faire would naturally eliminate corruption and monopolies.\nJ. They were proponents of complete government control over the economy.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{C}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 904, "output_tokens": 255, "total_tokens": 1159}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_economics_5951", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What is the difference between explicit and tacit collusion?\n\nOptions: \nA. Explicit collusion is an open agreement between companies on pricing, while tacit collusion implies similar pricing without any agreement.\nB. Explicit collusion involves a formal contract, while tacit collusion is based on informal social interactions.\nC. Explicit collusion is legal while tacit collusion is not\nD. Explicit collusion happens only in international markets, while tacit collusion is confined to domestic markets.\nE. Explicit collusion is based on verbal agreements, while tacit collusion relies on written contracts.\nF. Explicit collusion involves price fixing, while tacit collusion involves product quality fixation\nG. Explicit collusion occurs in retail markets, while tacit collusion occurs in wholesale markets.\nH. Explicit collusion requires physical meetings, while tacit collusion can be done online\nI. Explicit collusion is monitored by regulatory authorities, while tacit collusion goes undetected.\nJ. Explicit collusion is always done in secret, while tacit collusion is done openly.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{A}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1059, "output_tokens": 277, "total_tokens": 1336}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_economics_5811", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What is the main difference between the Dickey Fuller (DF) and Phillips-Perron (PP) approaches to unit root testing?\n\nOptions: \nA. The DF test incorporates an automatic correction for non-stationary data in the test regression\nB. PP test requires a priori knowledge of the lag length whereas DF test does not\nC. PP tests have good power in small samples whereas DF tests do not.\nD. ADF is a single equation approach to unit root testing while PP is a systems approach\nE. DF test is generally used for larger datasets, while the PP test is used for smaller ones\nF. PP tests assume the presence of unit root under the null hypothesis whereas DF tests do not\nG. PP tests reverse the DF null and alternative hypotheses so that there is stationarity under the null hypothesis of the PP test\nH. DF test requires larger sample size than the PP test for accurate results\nI. The PP test incorporates an automatic correction for autocorrelated residuals in the test regression\nJ. DF test is non-parametric whereas PP test is parametric\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the 