ext: None\n\nQuestion: Fiddler crabs are a dark brown color during the day and change to a pale brown color at night. Is it possible to reverse this pattern and make the crabs turn dark at night and light during the day? How?\n\nOptions: \nA. The crabs can be trained to change color on command using classical conditioning techniques.\nB. The use of hormonal treatments can induce a reverse in the crabs' color change pattern.\nC. Painting the crabs' shells can simulate the appearance of a reversed color pattern.\nD. The color change can be influenced by the presence of predators, causing a reverse pattern.\nE. The crabs can change color based on their mood.\nF. It is possible to reset this biological clock by artificially producing periods of light and darkness.\nG. The pattern is irreversible due to the crabs' hardwired circadian rhythms.\nH. The crabs can be forced to change color through genetic manipulation.\nI. The crabs' color change can be reversed through a diet change.\nJ. The color change can be triggered by altering the temperature of the crabs' environment.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{F}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1360, "output_tokens": 410, "total_tokens": 1770}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_biology_2856", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A woman opens a can of ham and beans and suspects food poisoning . She boils the contents for half an hour. A few hours after eating the ham and beans, nausea, vomit-ing, and diarrhea set in. Explain.\n\nOptions: \nA. The woman was poisoned by a toxin that came from a food handler's skin sores.\nB. The woman was poisoned by a toxin that is destroyed by boiling.\nC. The symptoms were due to psychological expectation of illness rather than actual food poisoning.\nD. The woman was poisoned by a pesticide residue on the can's surface.\nE. The woman's symptoms were caused by an allergic reaction to an ingredient in the ham and beans.\nF. The woman was poisoned by the botulinus toxin.\nG. The toxin produced by Staphylococcus aureus, which is heat-stable and unaffected by boiling, likely caused the poisoning.\nH. The ham and beans were contaminated with E. coli, which was not eliminated by boiling.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{G}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1204, "output_tokens": 394, "total_tokens": 1598}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_biology_3062", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In the early 1900's, many fruit growers made a practice of ripening fruits by keeping them in a room with a kerosene stove. They believed it was the heat that ripened the fruits. Was this belief based on correct facts? Explain your answer.\n\nOptions: \nA. The ripening was caused by the soot from the kerosene stove settling on the fruits.\nB. The fruits ripened due to the carbon dioxide produced by the kerosene stove.\nC. The fruits ripened faster due to increased humidity from the kerosene stove.\nD. It was the incomplete combustion products of the kerosene, identified as ethylene, that ripened the fruits.\nE. The fruits ripened naturally without any influence from the kerosene stove.\nF. It was the light emitted by the kerosene stove that accelerated the ripening process.\nG. The fruits ripened due to the ozone produced by the kerosene stove.\nH. The ripening was facilitated by the slight increase in temperature, not directly related to the heat of the stove.\nI. The kerosene stove released a specific ripening hormone that caused the fruits to ripen.\nJ. It was the heat from the kerosene stove that ripened the fruits.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{D}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1436, "output_tokens": 324, "total_tokens": 1760}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_biology_3277", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The citrate ion forms a tight bond with calcium ions so that the calcium is unable to react with other substances In view of this, why is sodium citrate added to whole blood immediately after it is drawn from a blood donor?\n\nOptions: \nA. Sodium citrate is added to cause muscle spasms\nB. Sodium citrate is added to catalyze the formation of fibrin\nC. Sodium citrate is added to preserve the blood's color\nD. Sodium citrate is added to stimulate immune responses\nE. Sodium citrate is added to increase calcium ions\nF. Sodium citrate is added to prevent coagulation\nG. Sodium citrate is added to increase the blood's viscosity\nH. Sodium citrate is added to enhance oxygen carrying capacity of the blood\nI. Sodium citrate is added to decrease the blood's pH level\nJ. Sodium citrate is added as a nutrient for blood cells\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{F}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1098, "output_tokens": 248, "total_tokens": 1346}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_biology_2715", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: About 0.5 percent of the American population have epilepsy, the falling sickness. Discuses the genetics of this disease.\n\nOptions: \nA. Epilepsy is the result of a chromosomal aneuploidy, similar to Down syndrome.\nB. Epilepsy is always inherited in an X-linked recessive pattern.\nC. Epilepsy is a dominant trait.\nD. Epilepsy is caused by an autosomal dominant trait.\nE. Epilepsy is due to an autosomal recessive trait.\nF. Epilepsy is caused by an autosomal dominant gene with complete penetrance.\nG. Epilepsy is a mitochondrial inheritance disorder.\nH. Epilepsy is exclusively caused by a single gene mutation.\nI. Epilepsy is not a genetic disorder.\nJ. Epilepsy is a polygenic disorder with no environmental factors involved.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{J}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1036, "output_tokens": 504, "total_tokens": 1540}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_biology_2860", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In attempting to map out the climatic conditions of the biosphere, would a climatologist find it helpful to correspond withan ecologist who has mapped the vegetation formations orbiomes of the biosphere?\n\nOptions: \nA. No, climatologists would find it more helpful to correspond with a geologist.\nB. Yes, however, it would be more beneficial to collaborate with a meteorologist.\nC. No, because the timescales on which vegetation formations change are too different from climatic shifts.\nD. Yes, but the information would be redundant since climate data already includes vegetation patterns.\nE. Yes, but only for specific types of vegetation.\nF. No, as ecologists' mapping is too detailed for the broad scales used in climatology.\nG. Yes, but only in areas where human activity has not altered the natural vegetation.\nH. Yes, vegetation-formation mapping does provide a basis for climatic mapping.\nI. No, because climatologists use satellite data exclusively for climatic mapping.\nJ. No, vegetation formations have no relation to climatic conditions.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{H}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1360, "output_tokens": 351, "total_tokens": 1711}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_biology_2984", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: When estimating the total number of Calories required by a personin a day, we consider such factors as sex, amount of activity, and the temperature in which the person lives. Why arethese factors necessary?\n\nOptions: \nA. Caloric requirements are only influenced by the environmental temperature, not by sex or activity level.\nB. Temperature has no effect on caloric requirements.\nC. Each person's Caloric requirement differs according to sex, amount of activity and environmental temperature.\nD. Caloric requirements are determined by age and sex only, without taking into account activity level or temperature.\nE. Only the sex of the person matters for caloric requirements.\nF. Caloric requirements are determined by a fixed formula that applies universally, regardless of individual differences.\nG. Caloric requirements decrease with increased activity and are independent of sex or environmental factors.\nH. Caloric requirement is the same for everyone.\nI. Caloric requirements increase only with the amount of activity and are unaffected by sex or temperature.\nJ. Caloric requirements are solely based on an individual's weight and height, with no consideration for sex, activity, or temperature.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{C}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1424, "output_tokens": 364, "total_tokens": 1788}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_biology_3218", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The muscle fibers innervated by a motor unit will contract if a stimulus of sufficient magnitude is propagated or it will not contract at all. This is known as the all-or- none law. If this is true, how can muscle force be graded? For example, we use the same muscles to lift a one ounce object that we use to lift a 20 lb. object.\n\nOptions: \nA. Muscle force is graded by the frequency of nerve impulses alone.\nB. Muscle force is graded by the weight of the object being lifted.\nC. Muscle force is graded by the amount of energy consumed.\nD. Muscle force is graded by altering the amount of blood flow to the muscle.\nE. Muscle force is graded by the duration of muscle fiber contraction.\nF. Muscle force is graded by the size of the muscle.\nG. Muscle force is graded by the type of muscle fiber being activated.\nH. Muscle force is graded by the number of motor units activated simultaneously.\nI. Muscle force can be graded through the process of recruitment and by varying the tension produced by individual fibers.\nJ. Muscle force is graded by the length of the muscle at the start of contraction.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "The correct answer is H, but also note that the more comprehensive description is I, as it includes both recruitment and rate coding. However, based on standard textbook phrasing and the options given, the most direct answer for grading force without violating the all-or-none law is by activating more motor units. Thus, \\boxed{H} is the best single-letter choice.", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1654, "output_tokens": 510, "total_tokens": 2164}, "model_used": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_biology_2985", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Why are magnolias found both in the eastern United States andeastern China, which belong to different realms, and hardlyanywhere else?\n\nOptions: \nA. Magnolias are found in these regions as a result of ancient trade routes that distributed seeds between the eastern United States and eastern China.\nB. Magnolias are found both in the eastern United States and eastern China due to the historical geographic continuity and warm moist climate of these regions, which were untouched by glaciation during the Cenozoic Era.\nC. The magnolias in these two regions were planted by humans.\nD. Magnolias are found in these regions due to the high biodiversity of both regions.\nE. The presence of magnolias in these regions is due to a shared climate zone that favors their growth, despite the distance.\nF. The magnolias in these regions are a result of convergent evolution, where similar species evolved independently in both areas.\nG. The distribution of magnolias is due to the accidental spread of seeds by human activities unrelated to intentional planting.\nH. Magnolias are native to only these two regions.\nI. Magnolias are found in these regions because they were transported there by migratory birds.\nJ. Magnolias thrive in both locations due to similar soil compositions found only in these two areas.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{B}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1343, "output_tokens": 365, "total_tokens": 1708}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_biology_3325", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Why is the drop in blood pressure important in retaining fluid within the capillaries?\n\nOptions: \nA. The drop in blood pressure causes blood clotting\nB. The drop in blood pressure causes an increase in plasma protein concentration\nC. The drop in blood pressure stimulates the excretion of fluids by the kidneys\nD. The drop in blood pressure causes dehydration\nE. The drop in blood pressure triggers the release of antidiuretic hormone\nF. The drop in blood pressure decreases the permeability of capillary walls\nG. The drop in blood pressure allows reabsorption of the lost fluid\nH. The drop in blood pressure increases the heart rate\nI. The drop in blood pressure leads to a constriction of the capillaries\nJ. The drop in blood pressure activates the sympathetic nervous system\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{G}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1088, "output_tokens": 352, "total_tokens": 1440}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_biology_2836", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: High levels of progesterone are essential for the maintenance of the uterine lining during pregnancy. But high levels of progesterone exerts a negative feedback action on LH secretion, with a consequent decrease in LH secretion and eventual atrophy of the corpus luteum. Atrophy of the corpus luteum cuts the progesterone supply resulting in menstruation. Clearly, these events cannot take place after conception, or the embryo would get sloughed off. When conception occurs, the corpus luteum is seen to last through most of pregnancy. How can this be explained?\n\nOptions: \nA. The endometrium produces a hormone that supports the corpus luteum throughout pregnancy.\nB. The body naturally adjusts progesterone levels during pregnancy.\nC. The placenta secretes a hormone, chorionic gonadotrophin, which takes the place of LH in preserving the corpus luteum. This allows the corpus luteum to secrete high levels of progesterone without being shut off.\nD. The corpus luteum spontaneously increases in size during pregnancy, which maintains progesterone levels without hormonal regulation.\nE. The corpus luteum is replaced by a secondary organ that secretes progesterone during pregnancy.\nF. The corpus luteum is not necessary during pregnancy.\nG. The embryo produces progesterone independently, eliminating the need for the corpus luteum after conception.\nH. The corpus luteum's lifespan is extended due to increased blood flow to the ovaries during pregnancy.\nI. The mother's pituitary gland secretes additional LH during pregnancy to maintain the corpus luteum.\nJ. The embryo secretes a hormone to preserve the corpus luteum.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{C}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1936, "output_tokens": 358, "total_tokens": 2294}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_biology_2736", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Within a few weeks of treatment with the drug 3TC, a patient's HIV population consists entirely of 3TC-resistant viruses. How can this result best be explained?\n\nOptions: \nA. The patient was initially infected with a 3TC-resistant strain of the virus.\nB. The patient's immune system developed a resistance to 3TC, causing the drug to become ineffective.\nC. The 3TC drug triggered a mutation in the HIV virus, causing it to become resistant.\nD. The patient's body started producing 3TC-resistant viruses in response to the drug treatment.\nE. A few drug-resistant viruses were present at the start of treatment, and natural selection increased their frequency.\nF. The HIV virus can naturally resist any form of drug treatment.\nG. The patient must have become reinfected with 3TC-resistant viruses.\nH. HIV began making drug-resistant versions of reverse transcriptase in response to the drug.\nI. The 3TC drug led to an increase in the production of HIV viruses.\nJ. HIV can change its surface proteins and resist vaccines.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{E}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1332, "output_tokens": 394, "total_tokens": 1726}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_biology_3154", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Why would it be disadvantageous for a host-specificparasite tokill its host?\n\nOptions: \nA. Parasites cannot harm their hosts\nB. Parasites are not affected by the health status of their host.\nC. Killing the host triggers an immune response that protects other potential hosts.\nD. Parasites always improve the health of their host for long-term survival.\nE. If they cause the extinction of their host, then they themselves would also become extinct.\nF. Host-specific parasites reproduce faster when their host is deceased.\nG. Host-specific parasites can easily find alternative hosts.\nH. Host-specific parasites can survive without a host\nI. Killing the host allows the parasite to spread more easily to new hosts.\nJ. All parasites are deadly to their hosts\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{E}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1068, "output_tokens": 354, "total_tokens": 1422}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_biology_2791", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Why would one call a flock of birds a \\textquotedblleftsociety\\textquotedblright?\n\nOptions: \nA. Birds form societies to find food\nB. A flock of birds can be considered an elementary form of society called a motion group. They communicate with one another in order to stay together as they move from place to place. The cooperative behavior enables them to more efficiently detect and avoid predators.\nC. A flock of birds is called a society because they display human-like social structures.\nD. Birds are classified as a society because they share a common language and culture.\nE. Birds form societies to perform complex problem-solving tasks as a group.\nF. Birds form societies to migrate together to the same location every year.\nG. Birds are considered a society because they are governed by a single leader.\nH. Birds form societies primarily for mating purposes.\nI. Birds form societies for social interaction\nJ. Birds form societies to build nests together\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{B}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1214, "output_tokens": 340, "total_tokens": 1554}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_biology_2640", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Mouse sperm contain receptor proteins that bind to a glycoprotein (ZP3) in the zona pellucida of mouse eggs. Binding of ZP3 receptors to ZP3 initiates the sperm\u2019s acrosomal reaction. All of the following experimental observations would be expected EXCEPT:\n\nOptions: \nA. Injecting eggs with antibodies that bind to ZP3 blocks in vitro fertilization.\nB. Incubating sperm with purified ZP3 prior to mixing sperm and eggs blocks in vitro fertilization.\nC. Incubating eggs with antibodies that bind to ZP3 blocks in vitro fertilization.\nD. Coating synthetic beads with a different glycoprotein, ZP2, fails to lead to sperm aggregation on the beads.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{A}  \n\nExplanation: Injecting eggs with antibodies that bind to ZP3 would not block fertilization because the antibodies would be inside the egg, not on the external zona pellucida where ZP3 interacts with sperm receptors. In contrast, incubating eggs externally with anti-ZP3 antibodies (option C) or pre-incubating sperm with purified ZP3 (option B) would block binding. The failure of ZP2-coated beads to aggregate sperm (option D) is also expected since ZP3 is the specific glycoprotein that binds sperm receptors.", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1135, "output_tokens": 1086, "total_tokens": 2221}, "model_used": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_biology_3134", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In the popular nursery rhyme \"Mary Had a Little Lamb\", her lambwould follow Mary wherever she went. Discuss a possi-blebiological explanation.\n\nOptions: \nA. The lamb had a habitual path that coincidentally aligned with Mary's route\nB. The lamb might have imprinted on Mary, a form of learning where the lamb forms a strong and lasting bond with the first moving object it sees.\nC. The lamb was guided by a shepherd and instructed to follow Mary\nD. The lamb was genetically programmed to follow Mary\nE. The lamb followed Mary due to a magnetic device she carried\nF. The lamb was trained to follow Mary\nG. The lamb followed Mary because of a distinctive sound she made\nH. The lamb was physically attached to Mary\nI. The lamb was attracted to the color of Mary's clothing\nJ. The lamb had been conditioned to follow a specific scent associated with Mary\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{B}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1120, "output_tokens": 346, "total_tokens": 1466}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_biology_3310", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Proteins were shown to move about in a plane of the plasma membrane when mouse cellsurface proteins and human cell-surface proteins were observed to integrate along a fused mouse-human cell plasma membrane. Which of the following cell culture techniques was most likely employed in order to yield these results?\n\nOptions: \nA. Inserting a bacterial plasmid into a mammalian cell\nB. Producing a hybrid cell\nC. Injecting a fluorescent marker into the cell membrane\nD. Producing a heterokaryon\nE. Isolating an immortal variant cell from culture and using it to create a cell line\nF. Inserting a tumor-inducing virus into a normal cell to initiate transformation\nG. Using a method called cell fusion to combine the mouse and human cells\nH. Using a technique called transfection to insert new genes\nI. Using a process called somatic cell nuclear transfer\nJ. Inducing pluripotency in somatic cells to create stem cells\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{G}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1048, "output_tokens": 317, "total_tokens": 1365}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_biology_2957", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Compare cardiac muscle to skeletal and smooth muscle.\n\nOptions: \nA. Cardiac muscle has a metabolism similar to smooth muscle, designed for quick, short bursts of activity.\nB. Cardiac muscle cells are multinucleated and arranged in a non-striated pattern.\nC. Cardiac muscle shows some characteristics of both skeletal and smooth muscle. It is striated, has numerous nuclei per cell, is innervated by the autonomic nervous system, has intercalated discs, and its metabolism is designed for endurance.\nD. Cardiac muscle lacks intercalated discs, which differentiates it from skeletal and smooth muscle.\nE. Cardiac muscle does not have any similarities with skeletal and smooth muscle\nF. Cardiac muscle is innervated by the somatic nervous system, similar to skeletal muscle.\nG. Cardiac muscle is similar to skeletal muscle but not smooth muscle\nH. Cardiac muscle has a metabolism designed for speed and strength\nI. Cardiac muscle is not striated and has a single nucleus per cell, similar to smooth muscle.\nJ. Cardiac muscle is identical to smooth muscle in both structure and function.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{C}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1396, "output_tokens": 414, "total_tokens": 1810}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_biology_2853", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A biology student from Nebraska, vacationing at a Virginia beach, observes that the level of activity of a fiddler crab changes with the cycle of the tides. When he brings some crabs back with him to Nebraska, he notices that a similar activity cycle is shown, yet there are no ocean beaches for miles. Explain.\n\nOptions: \nA. The level of activity is influenced by the ocean tides only\nB. The activity cycle is a genetic trait that persists regardless of the crabs' habitat\nC. The crabs' internal biological clocks are set to the tidal patterns they experienced in Virginia and continue on the same cycle\nD. Changes in barometric pressure in Nebraska are influencing the crabs' activity levels\nE. The level of activity is regulated by the crab's diet\nF. The crucial determinant of the level of activity of the crab is not the presence of the tides, but the gravitational pull of the moon\nG. The crabs are responding to the light cycles of day and night rather than the tides\nH. The crabs have adapted to a freshwater rhythmic cycle that mimics ocean tides\nI. The crabs are maintaining their Virginia activity schedule out of habit\nJ. The level of activity is being influenced by the temperature fluctuations in their new environment\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{C}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1544, "output_tokens": 406, "total_tokens": 1950}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_biology_3090", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Cellular uptake of cholesterol occurs through internalization of low density lipoproteincholesterol complexes. Cholesterol uptake involves all of the following EXCEPT\n\nOptions: \nA. cell-surface receptors\nB. adhesion plaques\nC. endosome formation\nD. receptor-mediated endocytosis\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{B}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 584, "output_tokens": 453, "total_tokens": 1037}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_biology_2911", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Why isn't a biome map of the earth a true representationof thevegetation formations found on land?\n\nOptions: \nA. Biome maps are outdated\nB. Biome maps are based solely on temperature, ignoring other ecological factors\nC. Biome maps classify vegetation based on animal population distribution rather than plant characteristics\nD. Biome maps are too detailed\nE. Biome maps use inaccurate color coding that does not reflect the true diversity of vegetation\nF. Real vegetation types usually grade one into another so it's hard to draw distinct boundaries\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{F}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 820, "output_tokens": 357, "total_tokens": 1177}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_biology_3197", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A proficient engineer can easily design skeletal structures that are more functional than those currently found in the forelimbs of such diverse mammals as horses, whales, and bats. That the actual forelimbs of these mammals do not seem to be optimally arranged is because\n\nOptions: \nA. the skeletal structures of these mammals are not designed for functionality, but for other purposes.\nB. these mammals have not evolved to their full potential yet.\nC. natural selection has not had sufficient time to create the optimal design in each case, but will do so given enough time.\nD. natural selection favors variety and diversity over optimal functionality.\nE. the complexity of the skeletal system hinders the possibility of optimal arrangement.\nF. natural selection is generally limited to modifying structures that were present in previous generations and in previous species.\nG. the skeletal structures are indeed optimally arranged, but our understanding of functionality is limited.\nH. optimal design is a human concept that does not apply to natural selection.\nI. in many cases, phenotype is not merely determined by genotype, but by the environment as well.\nJ. natural selection operates in ways that are beyond the capability of the human mind to comprehend.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{F}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1576, "output_tokens": 305, "total_tokens": 1881}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_biology_3322", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: An actress with O type blood accused a producer with B type blood of being the father of her child in a paternity suit. The child was also O type. What does the blood type suggest about his guilt?\n\nOptions: \nA. He cannot be the father\nB. The child's blood type suggests the mother has been unfaithful\nC. The child's blood type suggests a mutation occurred\nD. He could have been the father but it does not prove it\nE. The blood type proves he is the father because O type is recessive\nF. He definitely is the father\nG. He must have been the father because B type blood can produce O type offspring\nH. The blood type does not suggest anything\nI. The child's O type blood proves his innocence\nJ. He is probably not the father because B type blood cannot produce O type offspring\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{D}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1059, "output_tokens": 360, "total_tokens": 1419}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_biology_2964", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The dorsal root of a nerve leading to the leg of a rat is severed. How will this affect the rat? How would the effect vary if the ventral root were severed instead?\n\nOptions: \nA. Both severing the dorsal and ventral roots cause increased sensation and reflex action.\nB. Severing the dorsal root causes loss of motor function, severing the ventral root causes enhanced sensation.\nC. Both severing the dorsal and ventral roots lead to complete paralysis of the affected part.\nD. Severing the dorsal root causes enhanced movement, severing the ventral root has no effect.\nE. Severing the dorsal root causes loss of sensation and reflex action, severing the ventral root causes inability to move the affected part.\nF. Severing the dorsal root has no effect, severing the ventral root causes loss of sensation.\nG. Severing the dorsal root causes paralysis, severing the ventral root causes heightened sensitivity.\nH. Severing the dorsal root causes involuntary twitching, severing the ventral root causes loss of reflex action.\nI. Severing the dorsal root causes increased reflex action, severing the ventral root causes loss of sensation.\nJ. Severing the dorsal root has no effect, severing the ventral root causes paralysis.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{E}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1532, "output_tokens": 327, "total_tokens": 1859}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_biology_2658", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Can an extra Y chromosome (XXY) modify behavior?\n\nOptions: \nA. The extra Y chromosome decreases a male's aggression\nB. The extra Y chromosome is linked to an increase in artistic skills\nC. The extra Y chromosome is associated with a significant decrease in risk-taking behavior\nD. The extra Y chromosome leads to a decrease in physical strength\nE. The extra Y chromosome is responsible for a man's level of intelligence\nF. The extra Y chromosome results in a higher level of empathy in males\nG. The extra Y chromosome causes a substantial increase in memory capacity\nH. The extra Y chromosome may predispose a male to a more aggressive attitude\nI. The extra Y chromosome has no effect on a male's behavior\nJ. The extra Y chromosome enhances a male's cognitive abilities significantly\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{I}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1096, "output_tokens": 471, "total_tokens": 1567}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_biology_2788", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What is meant by translocation? What theories have been advanced to explain translocation in plants? Discuss the value and weakness of each.\n\nOptions: \nA. Translocation involves the movement of hormones within the plant; theories include the hormonal signaling theory and the vascular bundle theory.\nB. Translocation is the growth response of plants to gravity; theories include the gravitropism theory and the starch-statolith hypothesis.\nC. Translocation is the process by which plants absorb sunlight for photosynthesis; theories include light-chasing theory and phototropism.\nD. Translocation is explained by the xylem-flow theory\nE. Translocation is the movement of water in plants\nF. Translocation is the exchange of gases in plants; theories include the gas exchange theory and the stomatal pump theory.\nG. Translocation is the movement of nutrients from the leaves to other parts of the plant body. Theories to explain this process include the pressure-flow theory, the cyclosis theory, and the theory of cytoplasmic tubules.\nH. Translocation refers to the movement of plants from one location to another; theories include the wind-dispersal theory and water-dispersal theory.\nI. Translocation is the distribution of seeds by a plant; theories include the seed dispersal theory and the autochory theory.\nJ. Translocation involves the movement of nutrients from roots to leaves\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{G}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1618, "output_tokens": 376, "total_tokens": 1994}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_biology_2831", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: If all the chemical components found in a living cell were to beput together in the right proportion, the mixture would not functionas living matter. What characteristics would enable usto consider something living?\n\nOptions: \nA. Living things are characterized only by their complexity and organization, without the need for energy consumption or reproduction.\nB. Living things are characterized by a specific, complex organization, the ability to consume energy, movement, response to physical or chemical changes, growth, reproduction, and adaptability.\nC. Living things are characterized only by their growth.\nD. Living things are characterized by their ability to maintain homeostasis, without the need for growth, reproduction, or adaptability.\nE. Living things are characterized by their ability to evolve over generations but not by individual growth or energy consumption.\nF. Living things are characterized by their ability to respond to changes and growth, but not by reproduction or energy consumption.\nG. Living things are characterized only by their ability to consume energy.\nH. Living things are characterized only by their movement.\nI. Living things are characterized only by their adaptability to changes.\nJ. Living things are characterized only by their ability to reproduce.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter 