oxed{H}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1452, "output_tokens": 312, "total_tokens": 1764}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_biology_2946", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Can complete equilibrium in a gene pool exist in real situations?\n\nOptions: \nA. Genetic equilibrium can be achieved only through selective breeding\nB. Gene pool equilibrium is maintained exclusively by mutation\nC. Gene pool equilibrium is disrupted only by natural selection\nD. Only large populations can achieve genetic equilibrium\nE. Complete equilibrium in a gene pool is not expected\nF. Complete equilibrium in a gene pool can always be achieved\nG. Equilibrium can be achieved when there is no gene flow\nH. Equilibrium is guaranteed when there is a high mutation rate\nI. Complete equilibrium is possible if the population is isolated\nJ. Random reproduction is common in real situations\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{E}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 948, "output_tokens": 356, "total_tokens": 1304}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_biology_3070", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: If mating is observed to occur freely between organisms of twodifferent populations in the laboratory, and the cross producesviable offspring, can you assume that the two populationsbelong to the same species?\n\nOptions: \nA. Only if the offspring can also interbreed successfully.\nB. No, successful interbreeding in the laboratory must also be complemented by ecological, behavioral, and morphological studies to confirm they are the same species.\nC. It depends on whether the organisms can interbreed in a controlled environment other than a laboratory.\nD. No, successful interbreeding in the laboratory does not offer enough evidence that two populations must be of the same species.\nE. It depends on the type of organisms being tested.\nF. No, unless the interbreeding also occurs frequently in their natural habitat.\nG. Yes, successful interbreeding in the laboratory is sufficient proof of the same species.\nH. Only if the organisms share identical genetic markers.\nI. Yes, if the offspring show traits that are a mix of both parent populations.\nJ. Yes, as long as the offspring are not sterile.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{B}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1348, "output_tokens": 424, "total_tokens": 1772}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_biology_3158", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What is behavior? Are behavior patterns solely inherited or canthey be modified?\n\nOptions: \nA. Behavior patterns cannot be modified\nB. Behavior is limited to the animal kingdom\nC. Behavior is a random set of actions without any pattern or predictability\nD. Behavior patterns are determined exclusively by environmental factors without any genetic influence\nE. Behavior patterns are solely determined by immediate stimuli and are not influenced by past experiences\nF. Behavior patterns are fixed at birth and remain unchanged throughout an individual's life\nG. Behavior is an exclusively human trait not found in other animals\nH. Behavior is only learned through imitation and cannot be innate\nI. Both inheritance and learning are fundamental in the determination of behavioral patterns\nJ. Behavior patterns are solely inherited\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{I}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1140, "output_tokens": 329, "total_tokens": 1469}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_biology_3179", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: How might you argue that the transmission of impulses does occur in plants?\n\nOptions: \nA. Impulse transmission in plants can be seen in the photosynthesis process\nB. Plants exhibit impulse transmission during pollination, when they attract pollinators to their flowers.\nC. The release of chemical signals in response to herbivore damage, triggering defense mechanisms, is an example of impulse transmission in plants.\nD. The reaction of the sensitive plant, Mimosapudica, to touch demonstrates that transmission of impulses does indeed occur in plants.\nE. The opening and closing of stomata in response to environmental stimuli indicate impulse transmission in plants.\nF. The movement of chloroplasts within plant cells in response to light demonstrates impulse transmission.\nG. The transmission of impulses in plants can be observed through the growth of roots\nH. The process of fruit ripening, which involves a complex interplay of hormones, is a form of impulse transmission in plants.\nI. The bending of plant stems toward a light source, known as phototropism, is evidence of impulse transmission.\nJ. The seed germination process proves the transmission of impulses in plants\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{D}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1492, "output_tokens": 461, "total_tokens": 1953}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_biology_3215", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What experimental evidence indicates that genes control the aminoacid sequences in proteins?\n\nOptions: \nA. The amino acid sequence in a protein is not related to genetics.\nB. Direct observation of genes attaching amino acids to growing protein chains in live cells.\nC. Genes have no control over aminoacid sequences in proteins.\nD. Studies have shown that the removal of certain genes does not affect the amino acid sequence in proteins.\nE. Ingram's discovery was proof that a mutation in a gene resulted in an abnormal amino acid sequence in a protein.\nF. The presence of identical amino acid sequences in proteins from unrelated species.\nG. Aminoacid sequences in proteins are determined by environmental factors.\nH. The synthesis of amino acids in laboratory conditions without any genetic material involved.\nI. Ribosome malfunction has been shown to produce identical amino acid sequences in different proteins.