t letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{H}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 528, "output_tokens": 888, "total_tokens": 1416}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_math_6426", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Find all c in Z_3 such that Z_3[x]/(x^3 + x^2 + c) is a field.\n\nOptions: \nA. -2\nB. 2\nC. 1\nD. -1\nE. -3\nF. 0\nG. 3\nH. 4\nI. 6\nJ. 5\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "We are working in \\(\\mathbb{Z}_3\\), so the possible values for \\(c\\) are 0, 1, 2 (since \\(-1 = 2\\), \\(-2 = 1\\), etc.).  \nFor \\(\\mathbb{Z}_3[x]/(x^3+x^2+c)\\) to be a field, \\(x^3 + x^2 + c\\) must be irreducible over \\(\\mathbb{Z}_3\\).  \nChecking each: for \\(c=0\\), polynomial is \\(x^2(x+1)\\) (reducible); for \\(c=1\\), no root (0,1,2 not roots), so irreducible; for \\(c=2\\), root \\(x=1\\) gives \\(1+1+2 = 4\\equiv 1 \\neq 0\\)? Actually checking shows \\(x=1\\) gives \\(1+1+2=4\\equiv 1\\), \\(x=2\\) gives \\(8+4+2=14\\equiv 2\\), so no root means irreducible, but both \\(c=1\\) and \\(c=2\\) work. In \\(\\mathbb{Z}_3\\), \\(2\\) corresponds to \\(-1\\), so \\(-1\\) is among the answers.  \n\nThus the correct option is **D**.\n\n\\boxed{D}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 616, "output_tokens": 907, "total_tokens": 1523}, "model_used": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_math_7355", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Find integer $n \\ge 1$, such that $n \\cdot 2^{{n+1}}+1$ is a perfect square.\n\nOptions: \nA. 5\nB. 10\nC. 8\nD. 7\nE. 1\nF. 3\nG. 2\nH. 9\nI. 4\nJ. 6\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{F}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 487, "output_tokens": 345, "total_tokens": 832}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_math_7173", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A reading specialist in a large public school system believes that the more time students spend reading, the better they will do in school. She plans a middle school experiment in which an SRS of 30 eighth graders will be assigned four extra hours of reading per week, an SRS of 30 seventh graders will be assigned two extra hours of reading per week, and an SRS of 30 sixth graders with no extra assigned reading will be a control group. After one school year, the mean GPAs from each group will be compared. Is this a good experimental design?\n\nOptions: \nA. Yes, because the control group is necessary for comparison.\nB. Yes.\nC. No, because without blinding, there is a strong chance of a placebo effect.\nD. No, because grade level is a lurking variable which may well be confounded with the variables under consideration.\nE. No, because while this design may point out an association between reading and GPA, it cannot establish a cause-and-effect relationship.\nF. No, because the experiment does not account for the varying reading levels of the students.\nG. Yes, because the independent variable is manipulated by the researcher.\nH. No, because the experimental design does not include a double-blind procedure.\nI. Yes, because it tests the effect of different amounts of reading on GPA.\nJ. No, because the sample size of each group is not large enough.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{D}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 1420, "output_tokens": 276, "total_tokens": 1696}, "model_used": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "provider": "openrouter", "error": null}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "MMLUPro_math_7574", "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Let M be the set of bounded functions (i.e. \\sup_{{x\\in[a,b]}}|f(x)|<\\infty) in C[0,1]. Is the set ${{F(x)=\\int_0^x f(t) dt | f \\in M }}$ a sequentially compact set? Answer 1 for yes and 0 for no. Furthermore, it can be proved using 1. Arzel\u00e0-Ascoli theorem, 2. Riesz representation theorem, 3. Banach fixed point theorem, 4. None of the above. Return the answers of the two questions in a list. For example, if you think the answer is no and Riesz representation theorem, then return [0,2].\n\nOptions: \nA. [0, 2]\nB. [0, 3]\nC. [1, 3]\nD. [0, 1]\nE. [1, 1]\nF. [1, 0]\nG. [1, 2]\nH. [0, 4]\nI. [1, 4]\nJ. [0, 0]\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507", "generated_result": {"gene